{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-001", "content_item_id": "3bf61cc2-4fc9-4924-b0ca-def859e7f531", "content_item_version_id": "86e72fa9-ccf0-4496-aba2-ef42f8ec8fe1", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nv=6.00 m/s and Δx=12.0 m from constant-acceleration relations.\n\n(a) With v0=0, v=v0+at=0+(1.50)(4.00)=6.00 m/s, and Δx=v0t+(1/2)at²=0+(1/2)(1.50)(4.00)²=12.0 m. (b) These constant-acceleration equations apply because a is constant. Newton's second law gives F_net=ma, so constant mass and acceleration require a constant net force.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "v=6.00 m/s and Δx=12.0 m from constant-acceleration relations.", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "recovered", "source_field": "prior_canonical_answer_1", "source_offset": {"start": 0, "end": 62}}, {"text": "\n\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) With v0=0, v=v0+at=0+(1.50)(4.00)=6.00 m/s, and Δx=v0t+(1/2)at²=0+(1/2)(1.50)(4.00)²=12.0 m. (b) These constant-acceleration equations apply because a is constant. Newton's second law gives F_net=ma, so constant mass and acceleration require a constant net force.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 2, "criteria_covered": ["part-a", "part-b"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "6.00", "criterion_keys": [], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "v=6.00 m/s and Δx=12.0 m from constant-acceleration relations.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Derive the cart's velocity and displacement after 4.00 s of uniform acceleration, and evaluate their numeric values.\n\n(b) Explain how the constant-acceleration kinematics equations, together with the constant net force required by Newton's second law for the acceleration to remain constant, justify the computed velocity and displacement. A cart starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 1.50 m/s² for 4.00 s."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "12.0", "criterion_keys": [], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "v=6.00 m/s and Δx=12.0 m from constant-acceleration relations.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Derive the cart's velocity and displacement after 4.00 s of uniform acceleration, and evaluate their numeric values.\n\n(b) Explain how the constant-acceleration kinematics equations, together with the constant net force required by Newton's second law for the acceleration to remain constant, justify the computed velocity and displacement. A cart starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 1.50 m/s² for 4.00 s."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) With v0=0, v=v0+at=0+(1.50)(4.00)=6.00 m/s, and Δx=v0t+(1/2)at²=0+(1/2)(1.50)(4.00)²=12.0 m. (b) These constant-acceleration equations apply because a is constant. Newton's second law gives F_net=ma, so constant mass and acceleration require a constant net force.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Derive the cart's velocity and displacement after 4.00 s of uniform acceleration, and evaluate their numeric values.\n\n(b) Explain how the constant-acceleration kinematics equations, together with the constant net f