{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-012", "content_item_id": "da3c6768-3b04-40bc-9beb-9ba398447f47", "content_item_version_id": "d5148959-28b2-4e2d-a111-f64926d3d2f4", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nMomentum conservation gives final speed 2.00 m/s; kinetic energy decreases.\n\n(a) I would vary the incoming cart's speed (independent variable), measure the stuck carts' final velocity (dependent variable), and keep the initially stationary cart's mass fixed. For several incoming speeds, photogates measure both carts immediately before and after collision; for each trial compare total momentum before and after. (b) During the short collision the external horizontal impulse is negligible, so system momentum is conserved: (2.00)(3.00)+(1.00)(0)=(3.00)v_f, giving v_f=2.00 m/s. Because the carts stick, deformation and thermal/internal energy increase, so kinetic energy is not conserved.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "Momentum conservation gives final speed 2.00 m/s; kinetic energy decreases.", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "recovered", "source_field": "prior_canonical_answer_1", "source_offset": {"start": 0, "end": 75}}, {"text": "\n\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) I would vary the incoming cart's speed (independent variable), measure the stuck carts' final velocity (dependent variable), and keep the initially stationary cart's mass fixed. For several incoming speeds, photogates measure both carts immediately before and after collision; for each trial compare total momentum before and after. (b) During the short collision the external horizontal impulse is negligible, so system momentum is conserved: (2.00)(3.00)+(1.00)(0)=(3.00)v_f, giving v_f=2.00 m/s. Because the carts stick, deformation and thermal/internal energy increase, so kinetic energy is not conserved.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 2, "criteria_covered": ["part-a", "part-b"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "2.00", "criterion_keys": [], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "Momentum conservation gives final speed 2.00 m/s; kinetic energy decreases.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a feasible measurement that tests conservation of momentum; identify the independent variable, dependent variable, one control, and briefly describe your experimental procedure.\n\n(b) Explain why treating the collision as perfectly inelastic with no external horizontal impulse justifies using momentum conservation to find the final speed, and why kinetic energy is not conserved. A 2.00 kg cart at 3.00 m/s sticks to a 1.00 kg cart initially at rest."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2.00", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) I would vary the incoming cart's speed (independent variable), measure the stuck carts' final velocity (dependent variable), and keep the initially stationary cart's mass fixed. For several incoming speeds, photogates measure both carts immediately before and after collision; for each trial compare total momentum before and after. (b) During the short collision the external horizontal impulse is negligible, so system momentum is conserved: (2.00)(3.00)+(1.00)(0)=(3.00)v_f, giving v_f=2.00 m/s. Because the carts stick, deformation and thermal/internal energy increase, so kinetic energy is not conserved.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a feasible measurement that tests conservation of momentum; identify the independent variable, dependent variable, one control, and briefly describe your experimental procedure.\n\n(b) Explain why treating the collision as perfectly inelastic with no external horizontal impulse justifies using momentum conservation to find the final speed, and why kinetic energy is not conserved. A 2.00 kg cart at 3.00 m/s sticks to a 1.00 kg cart initially at rest."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "3.00", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) I would vary the incoming cart's speed (independent variable), measure the stuck carts' final velocity (dependent variable), and keep the initially stationary cart's mass fixed. For several incoming speeds, photogates measure both carts immediately before and after collision; for each trial compare total momentum before and after. (b) During the short collision the external horizontal impulse is negligible, so system momentum is conserved: (2.00)(3.00)+(1.00)(0)=(3.00)v_f, giving v_f=2.00 m/s. Because the carts stick, deformation and thermal/internal energy increase, so kinetic energy is not conserved.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a feasible measurement that tests conservation of momentum; identify the independent variable, dependent variable, one control, and briefly describe your experimental procedure.\n\n(b) Explain why treating the collision as perfectly inelastic with no external horizontal impulse justifies using momentum conservation to find the final speed, and why kinetic energy is not conserved. A 2.00 kg cart at 3.00 m/s sticks to a 1.00 kg cart initially at rest."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1.00", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) I would vary the incoming cart's speed (independent variable), measure the stuck carts' final velocity (dependent variable), and keep the initially stationary cart's mass fixed. For several incoming speeds, photogates measure both carts immediately before and after collision; for each trial compare total momentum before and after. (b) During the short collision the external horizontal impulse is negligible, so system momentum is conserved: (2.00)(3.00)+(1.00)(0)=(3.00)v_f, giving v_f=2.00 m/s. Because the carts stick, deformation and thermal/internal energy increase, so kinetic energy is not conserved.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a feasible measurement that tests conservation of momentum; identify the independent variable, dependent variable, one control, and briefly describe your experimental procedure.\n\n(b) Explain why treating the collision as perfectly inelastic with no external horizontal impulse justifies using momentum conservation to find the final speed, and why kinetic energy is not conserved. A 2.00 kg cart at 3.00 m/s sticks to a 1.00 kg cart initially at rest."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) I would vary the incoming cart's speed (independent variable), measure the stuck carts' final velocity (dependent variable), and keep the initially stationary cart's mass fixed. For several incoming speeds, photogates measure both carts immediately before and after collision; for each trial compare total momentum before and after. (b) During the short collision the external horizontal impulse is negligible, so system momentum is conserved: (2.00)(3.00)+(1.00)(0)=(3.00)v_f, giving v_f=2.00 m/s. Because the carts stick, deformation and thermal/internal energy increase, so kinetic energy is not conserved.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a feasible measurement that tests conservation of momentum; identify the independent variable, dependent variable, one control, and briefly describe your experimental procedure.\n\n(b) Explain why treating the collision as perfectly inelastic with no external horizontal impulse justifies using momentum conservation to find the final speed, and why kinetic energy is not conserved. A 2.00 kg cart at 3.00 m/s sticks to a 1.00 kg cart initially at rest."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "3.00", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) I would vary the incoming cart's speed (independent variable), measure the stuck carts' final velocity (dependent variable), and keep the initially stationary cart's mass fixed. For several incoming speeds, photogates measure both carts immediately before and after collision; for each trial compare total momentum before and after. (b) During the short collision the external horizontal impulse is negligible, so system momentum is conserved: (2.00)(3.00)+(1.00)(0)=(3.00)v_f, giving v_f=2.00 m/s. Because the carts stick, deformation and thermal/internal energy increase, so kinetic energy is not conserved.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a feasible measurement that tests conservation of momentum; identify the independent variable, dependent variable, one control, and briefly describe your experimental procedure.\n\n(b) Explain why treating the collision as perfectly inelastic with no external horizontal impulse justifies using momentum conservation to find the final speed, and why kinetic energy is not conserved. A 2.00 kg cart at 3.00 m/s sticks to a 1.00 kg cart initially at rest."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2.00", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) I would vary the incoming cart's speed (independent variable), measure the stuck carts' final velocity (dependent variable), and keep the initially stationary cart's mass fixed. For several incoming speeds, photogates measure both carts immediately before and after collision; for each trial compare total momentum before and after. (b) During the short collision the external horizontal impulse is negligible, so system momentum is conserved: (2.00)(3.00)+(1.00)(0)=(3.00)v_f, giving v_f=2.00 m/s. Because the carts stick, deformation and thermal/internal energy increase, so kinetic energy is not conserved.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a feasible measurement that tests conservation of momentum; identify the independent variable, dependent variable, one control, and briefly describe your experimental procedure.\n\n(b) Explain why treating the collision as perfectly inelastic with no external horizontal impulse justifies using momentum conservation to find the final speed, and why kinetic energy is not conserved. A 2.00 kg cart at 3.00 m/s sticks to a 1.00 kg cart initially at rest."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-018", "content_item_id": "e93a861c-69c7-4452-9e88-eb661dd3a204", "content_item_version_id": "6bc66fdd-2dfe-43ca-8f24-f5c33694d9ac", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nNet force = 0: F_buoyant = ρ_water g V (up), weight = ρ_block g V (down), tension T (down since ρ_block < ρ_water); T = (ρ_water − ρ_block) g V, pulling the block downward toward the anchor.\n\n(a) The free-body diagram has buoyancy F_B=ρ_water gV upward, weight W=ρ_block gV downward, and tension T downward. Equilibrium gives ΣF_y=F_B-W-T=0. (b) Since ρ_block<ρ_water, F_B>W and a downward tension T=(ρ_water-ρ_block)gV is required. A string can pull downward only toward an anchor below the block, so the anchor must be at the tank bottom.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "Net force = 0: F_buoyant = ρ_water g V (up), weight = ρ_block g V (down), tension T (down since ρ_block < ρ_water); T = (ρ_water − ρ_block) g V, pulling the block downward toward the anchor.", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "recovered", "source_field": "prior_canonical_answer_1", "source_offset": {"start": 0, "end": 190}}, {"text": "\n\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) The free-body diagram has buoyancy F_B=ρ_water gV upward, weight W=ρ_block gV downward, and tension T downward. Equilibrium gives ΣF_y=F_B-W-T=0. (b) Since ρ_block<ρ_water, F_B>W and a downward tension T=(ρ_water-ρ_block)gV is required. A string can pull downward only toward an anchor below the block, so the anchor must be at the tank bottom.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 2, "criteria_covered": ["part-a", "part-b"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "0", "criterion_keys": [], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "Net force = 0: F_buoyant = ρ_water g V (up), weight = ρ_block g V (down), tension T (down since ρ_block < ρ_water); T = (ρ_water − ρ_block) g V, pulling the block downward toward the anchor.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Draw a free-body diagram of the block and write Newton's second law for the block in equilibrium, including the buoyant force, gravity, and the string tension.\n\n(b) Using Archimedes' principle, determine whether the string's anchor point must be below the block (at the tank bottom) or above the block (at the water's surface or tank top) for the block to remain in equilibrium, and justify your answer in terms of ρ_block and ρ_water. A solid block of volume V and density ρ_block is fully submerged in water of density ρ_water and held stationary by a taut string connected to a fixed anchor point in the tank. The block's density is less than the density of water."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) The free-body diagram has buoyancy F_B=ρ_water gV upward, weight W=ρ_block gV downward, and tension T downward. Equilibrium gives ΣF_y=F_B-W-T=0. (b) Since ρ_block<ρ_water, F_B>W and a downward tension T=(ρ_water-ρ_block)gV is required. A string can pull downward only toward an anchor below the block, so the anchor must be at the tank bottom.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Draw a free-body diagram of the block and write Newton's second law for the block in equilibrium, including the buoyant force, gravity, and the string tension.\n\n(b) Using Archimedes' principle, determine whether the string's anchor point must be below the block (at the tank bottom) or above the block (at the water's surface or tank top) for the block to remain in equilibrium, and justify your answer in terms of ρ_block and ρ_water. A solid block of volume V and density ρ_block is fully submerged in water of density ρ_water and held stationary by a taut string connected to a fixed anchor point in the tank. The block's density is less than the density of water."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-024", "content_item_id": "18e6f11e-30eb-4a83-ae6e-d685b1a2483a", "content_item_version_id": "5bb16d64-6210-499b-aeb7-fa59823ac11a", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nWith no external horizontal force, center-of-mass velocity is unchanged. The impulses and momentum changes are equal and opposite; the lighter cart's speed change is twice the heavier cart's.\n\n(a) The center-of-mass velocity stays constant because the horizontal external force is zero; the spring forces are internal, so total momentum is conserved. (b) The spring gives equal-magnitude, opposite impulses, so |Δp_light|=|Δp_heavy|. Since |Δv|=|Δp|/m, if the heavy cart has twice the light cart's mass, the light cart's velocity change is twice the heavy cart's.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "With no external horizontal force, center-of-mass velocity is unchanged. The impulses and momentum changes are equal and opposite; the lighter cart's speed change is twice the heavier cart's.", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "recovered", "source_field": "prior_canonical_answer_1", "source_offset": {"start": 0, "end": 191}}, {"text": "\n\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) The center-of-mass velocity stays constant because the horizontal external force is zero; the spring forces are internal, so total momentum is conserved. (b) The spring gives equal-magnitude, opposite impulses, so |Δp_light|=|Δp_heavy|. Since |Δv|=|Δp|/m, if the heavy cart has twice the light cart's mass, the light cart's velocity change is twice the heavy cart's.", "criterion_keys": ["a-com", "b-momentum", "b-speed"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 3, "criteria_covered": ["a-com", "b-momentum", "b-speed"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-032", "content_item_id": "4d76ab52-1652-497e-9c3d-214686de5aa9", "content_item_version_id": "b95a39b7-e1be-49ae-aa09-a5f9dbd5cd01", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nUse F_1+F_2=mg and torque balance about either support, such as F_2(x_2-x_1)=mg(x_cm-x_1), then repeat with new support positions.\n\n(a) With the stick stationary, measure support positions x1=20 cm and x2=80 cm and one support force F1. Infer F2 from F1+F2=mg. (b) Taking torques about x1 eliminates F1 and gives F2(x2-x1)=mg(x_cm-x1), so x_cm=x1+F2(x2-x1)/(mg). (c) The result must lie between 20 cm and 80 cm and, when substituted back, satisfy both force and torque balance. Repeat with different support positions and average mutually consistent x_cm values to reduce random uncertainty.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "Use F_1+F_2=mg and torque balance about either support, such as F_2(x_2-x_1)=mg(x_cm-x_1), then repeat with new support positions.", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "recovered", "source_field": "prior_canonical_answer_1", "source_offset": {"start": 0, "end": 130}}, {"text": "\n\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) With the stick stationary, measure support positions x1=20 cm and x2=80 cm and one support force F1. Infer F2 from F1+F2=mg. (b) Taking torques about x1 eliminates F1 and gives F2(x2-x1)=mg(x_cm-x1), so x_cm=x1+F2(x2-x1)/(mg). (c) The result must lie between 20 cm and 80 cm and, when substituted back, satisfy both force and torque balance. Repeat with different support positions and average mutually consistent x_cm values to reduce random uncertainty.", "criterion_keys": ["a-infer", "a-measure", "b-pivot", "b-torque", "c-check", "c-uncertainty"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 6, "criteria_covered": ["a-infer", "a-measure", "b-pivot", "b-torque", "c-check", "c-uncertainty"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "20", "criterion_keys": ["a-infer", "a-measure", "b-pivot", "b-torque", "c-check", "c-uncertainty"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) With the stick stationary, measure support positions x1=20 cm and x2=80 cm and one support force F1. Infer F2 from F1+F2=mg. (b) Taking torques about x1 eliminates F1 and gives F2(x2-x1)=mg(x_cm-x1), so x_cm=x1+F2(x2-x1)/(mg). (c) The result must lie between 20 cm and 80 cm and, when substituted back, satisfy both force and torque balance. Repeat with different support positions and average mutually consistent x_cm values to reduce random uncertainty.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a procedure to locate the stick's center of mass using equilibrium measurements.\n\n(b) Write the force and torque relationships needed.\n\n(c) Describe a consistency check and one uncertainty-reduction method. A nonuniform meterstick is supported horizontally at the 20 cm and 80 cm marks. A force sensor measures one support force; the stick's mass is known."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "80", "criterion_keys": ["a-infer", "a-measure", "b-pivot", "b-torque", "c-check", "c-uncertainty"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) With the stick stationary, measure support positions x1=20 cm and x2=80 cm and one support force F1. Infer F2 from F1+F2=mg. (b) Taking torques about x1 eliminates F1 and gives F2(x2-x1)=mg(x_cm-x1), so x_cm=x1+F2(x2-x1)/(mg). (c) The result must lie between 20 cm and 80 cm and, when substituted back, satisfy both force and torque balance. Repeat with different support positions and average mutually consistent x_cm values to reduce random uncertainty.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a procedure to locate the stick's center of mass using equilibrium measurements.\n\n(b) Write the force and torque relationships needed.\n\n(c) Describe a consistency check and one uncertainty-reduction method. A nonuniform meterstick is supported horizontally at the 20 cm and 80 cm marks. A force sensor measures one support force; the stick's mass is known."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "20", "criterion_keys": ["a-infer", "a-measure", "b-pivot", "b-torque", "c-check", "c-uncertainty"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) With the stick stationary, measure support positions x1=20 cm and x2=80 cm and one support force F1. Infer F2 from F1+F2=mg. (b) Taking torques about x1 eliminates F1 and gives F2(x2-x1)=mg(x_cm-x1), so x_cm=x1+F2(x2-x1)/(mg). (c) The result must lie between 20 cm and 80 cm and, when substituted back, satisfy both force and torque balance. Repeat with different support positions and average mutually consistent x_cm values to reduce random uncertainty.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a procedure to locate the stick's center of mass using equilibrium measurements.\n\n(b) Write the force and torque relationships needed.\n\n(c) Describe a consistency check and one uncertainty-reduction method. A nonuniform meterstick is supported horizontally at the 20 cm and 80 cm marks. A force sensor measures one support force; the stick's mass is known."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "80", "criterion_keys": ["a-infer", "a-measure", "b-pivot", "b-torque", "c-check", "c-uncertainty"], "span_index": 3, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) With the stick stationary, measure support positions x1=20 cm and x2=80 cm and one support force F1. Infer F2 from F1+F2=mg. (b) Taking torques about x1 eliminates F1 and gives F2(x2-x1)=mg(x_cm-x1), so x_cm=x1+F2(x2-x1)/(mg). (c) The result must lie between 20 cm and 80 cm and, when substituted back, satisfy both force and torque balance. Repeat with different support positions and average mutually consistent x_cm values to reduce random uncertainty.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design a procedure to locate the stick's center of mass using equilibrium measurements.\n\n(b) Write the force and torque relationships needed.\n\n(c) Describe a consistency check and one uncertainty-reduction method. A nonuniform meterstick is supported horizontally at the 20 cm and 80 cm marks. A force sensor measures one support force; the stick's mass is known."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-033", "content_item_id": "14b30ea9-0539-4911-8910-47ffd6d5c60e", "content_item_version_id": "beacb3d2-fd4c-404d-95b9-e734637d17d8", "version_num": 3, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) One-period functions that specify the three aligned graphs are x=A cos(2πt/T), v=-(2πA/T)sin(2πt/T), and a=-(2π/T)²A cos(2πt/T). Thus x starts at +A, v starts at zero and becomes negative, and a starts at its most negative value; all share period T. (b) Because a=-ω²x, acceleration is opposite position. Velocity is one-quarter period out of phase with position; |v| and kinetic energy are maximum at x=0, at t=T/4 and 3T/4.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) One-period functions that specify the three aligned graphs are x=A cos(2πt/T), v=-(2πA/T)sin(2πt/T), and a=-(2π/T)²A cos(2πt/T). Thus x starts at +A, v starts at zero and becomes negative, and a starts at its most negative value; all share period T. (b) Because a=-ω²x, acceleration is opposite position. Velocity is one-quarter period out of phase with position; |v| and kinetic energy are maximum at x=0, at t=T/4 and 3T/4.", "criterion_keys": ["a-graphs", "b-energy", "b-phase"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 3, "criteria_covered": ["a-graphs", "b-energy", "b-phase"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-graphs", "b-energy", "b-phase"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) One-period functions that specify the three aligned graphs are x=A cos(2πt/T), v=-(2πA/T)sin(2πt/T), and a=-(2π/T)²A cos(2πt/T). Thus x starts at +A, v starts at zero and becomes negative, and a starts at its most negative value; all share period T. (b) Because a=-ω²x, acceleration is opposite position. Velocity is one-quarter period out of phase with position; |v| and kinetic energy are maximum at x=0, at t=T/4 and 3T/4.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Sketch position, velocity, and acceleration versus time for one period with aligned time axes.\n\n(b) Explain the phase relationships and identify when kinetic energy is maximum. A mass-spring oscillator starts at maximum positive displacement A at t=0 and has period T."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-graphs", "b-energy", "b-phase"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) One-period functions that specify the three aligned graphs are x=A cos(2πt/T), v=-(2πA/T)sin(2πt/T), and a=-(2π/T)²A cos(2πt/T). Thus x starts at +A, v starts at zero and becomes negative, and a starts at its most negative value; all share period T. (b) Because a=-ω²x, acceleration is opposite position. Velocity is one-quarter period out of phase with position; |v| and kinetic energy are maximum at x=0, at t=T/4 and 3T/4.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Sketch position, velocity, and acceleration versus time for one period with aligned time axes.\n\n(b) Explain the phase relationships and identify when kinetic energy is maximum. A mass-spring oscillator starts at maximum positive displacement A at t=0 and has period T."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-graphs", "b-energy", "b-phase"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) One-period functions that specify the three aligned graphs are x=A cos(2πt/T), v=-(2πA/T)sin(2πt/T), and a=-(2π/T)²A cos(2πt/T). Thus x starts at +A, v starts at zero and becomes negative, and a starts at its most negative value; all share period T. (b) Because a=-ω²x, acceleration is opposite position. Velocity is one-quarter period out of phase with position; |v| and kinetic energy are maximum at x=0, at t=T/4 and 3T/4.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Sketch position, velocity, and acceleration versus time for one period with aligned time axes.\n\n(b) Explain the phase relationships and identify when kinetic energy is maximum. A mass-spring oscillator starts at maximum positive displacement A at t=0 and has period T."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-graphs", "b-energy", "b-phase"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) One-period functions that specify the three aligned graphs are x=A cos(2πt/T), v=-(2πA/T)sin(2πt/T), and a=-(2π/T)²A cos(2πt/T). Thus x starts at +A, v starts at zero and becomes negative, and a starts at its most negative value; all share period T. (b) Because a=-ω²x, acceleration is opposite position. Velocity is one-quarter period out of phase with position; |v| and kinetic energy are maximum at x=0, at t=T/4 and 3T/4.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Sketch position, velocity, and acceleration versus time for one period with aligned time axes.\n\n(b) Explain the phase relationships and identify when kinetic energy is maximum. A mass-spring oscillator starts at maximum positive displacement A at t=0 and has period T."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-graphs", "b-energy", "b-