has period T."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "4", "criterion_keys": ["a-graphs", "b-energy", "b-phase"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) One-period functions that specify the three aligned graphs are x=A cos(2πt/T), v=-(2πA/T)sin(2πt/T), and a=-(2π/T)²A cos(2πt/T). Thus x starts at +A, v starts at zero and becomes negative, and a starts at its most negative value; all share period T. (b) Because a=-ω²x, acceleration is opposite position. Velocity is one-quarter period out of phase with position; |v| and kinetic energy are maximum at x=0, at t=T/4 and 3T/4.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Sketch position, velocity, and acceleration versus time for one period with aligned time axes.\n\n(b) Explain the phase relationships and identify when kinetic energy is maximum. A mass-spring oscillator starts at maximum positive displacement A at t=0 and has period T."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "4", "criterion_keys": ["a-graphs", "b-energy", "b-phase"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) One-period functions that specify the three aligned graphs are x=A cos(2πt/T), v=-(2πA/T)sin(2πt/T), and a=-(2π/T)²A cos(2πt/T). Thus x starts at +A, v starts at zero and becomes negative, and a starts at its most negative value; all share period T. (b) Because a=-ω²x, acceleration is opposite position. Velocity is one-quarter period out of phase with position; |v| and kinetic energy are maximum at x=0, at t=T/4 and 3T/4.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Sketch position, velocity, and acceleration versus time for one period with aligned time axes.\n\n(b) Explain the phase relationships and identify when kinetic energy is maximum. A mass-spring oscillator starts at maximum positive displacement A at t=0 and has period T."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "medium", "flags": ["textual_graph_or_diagram_representation", "cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-035", "content_item_id": "de650c07-ac1b-40cd-a4cd-2c17633ed7c3", "content_item_version_id": "9f159e00-a8d9-4690-945b-c255632a5429", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) Energy with friction is E_i+W_nc=E_f: (1/2)kx²-μmgL=mgh, because f_k=μN=μmg, W_f=-μmgL, and the block has zero kinetic energy at maximum height. Therefore h=kx²/(2mg)-μL. The block reaches the ramp only if kx²/2>μmgL. (b) For compression 2x, h(2x)=k(2x)²/(2mg)-μL=2kx²/(mg)-μL. Hence h(2x)-h(x)=3kx²/(2mg). Spring energy scales as x² while the frictional loss is unchanged.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) Energy with friction is E_i+W_nc=E_f: (1/2)kx²-μmgL=mgh, because f_k=μN=μmg, W_f=-μmgL, and the block has zero kinetic energy at maximum height. Therefore h=kx²/(2mg)-μL. The block reaches the ramp only if kx²/2>μmgL. (b) For compression 2x, h(2x)=k(2x)²/(2mg)-μL=2kx²/(mg)-μL. Hence h(2x)-h(x)=3kx²/(2mg). Spring energy scales as x² while the frictional loss is unchanged.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-a-criterion-03", "part-a-criterion-04", "part-a-criterion-05", "part-a-criterion-06", "part-a-criterion-07", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-03"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 10, "criteria_covered": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-a-criterion-03", "part-a-criterion-04", "part-a-criterion-05", "part-a-criterion-06", "part-a-criterion-07", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-03"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "1", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-a-criterion-03", "part-a-criterion-04", "part-a-criterion-05", "part-a-criterion-06", "part-a-criterion-07", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-03"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) Energy with friction is E_i+W_nc=E_f: (1/2)kx²-μmgL=mgh, because f_k=μN=μmg, W_f=-μmgL, and the block has zero kinetic energy at maximum height. Therefore h=kx²/(2mg)-μL. The block reaches the ramp only if kx²/2>μmgL. (b) For compression 2x, h(2x)=k(2x)²/(2mg)-μL=2kx²/(mg)-μL. Hence h(2x)-h(x)=3kx²/(2mg). Spring energy scales as x² while the frictional loss is unchanged.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Spring launch on a rough track: Answer all mandatory subparts. Show reasoning and calculations clearly. Use standard AP Physics notation and justify qualitative claims. A block of mass m is released from rest against a spring of constant k compressed a distance x. After losing contact with the spring, the block crosses a horizontal rough region of length L with kinetic-friction coefficient μ and then moves up a frictionless ramp. The zero of gravitational potential energy is the horizontal track. Express answers in terms of the given symbols and g."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-a-criterion-03", "part-a-criterion-04", "part-a-criterion-05", "part-a-criterion-06", "part-a-criterion-07", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-03"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) Energy with friction is E_i+W_nc=E_f: (1/2)kx²-μmgL=mgh, because f_k=μN=μmg, W_f=-μmgL, and the block has zero kinetic energy at maximum height. Therefore h=kx²/(2mg)-μL. The block reaches the ramp only if kx²/2>μmgL. (b) For compression 2x, h(2x)=k(2x)²/(2mg)-μL=2kx²/(mg)-μL. Hence h(2x)-h(x)=3kx²/(2mg). Spring energy scales as x² while the frictional loss is unchanged.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Spring launch on a rough track: Answer all mandatory subparts. Show reasoning and calculations clearly. Use standard AP Physics notation and justify qualitative claims. A block of mass m is released from rest against a spring of constant k compressed a distance x. After losing contact with the spring, the block crosses a horizontal rough region of length L with kinetic-friction coefficient μ and then moves up a frictionless ramp. The zero of gravitational potential energy is the horizontal track. Express answers in terms of the given symbols and g."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-a-criterion-03", "part-a-criterion-04", "part-a-criterion-05", "part-a-criterion-06", "part-a-criterion-07", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-03"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) Energy with friction is E_i+W_nc=E_f: (1/2)kx²-μmgL=mgh, because f_k=μN=μmg, W_f=-μmgL, and the block has zero kinetic energy at maximum height. Therefore h=kx²/(2mg)-μL. The block reaches the ramp only if kx²/2>μmgL. (b) For compression 2x, h(2x)=k(2x)²/(2mg)-μL=2kx²/(mg)-μL. Hence h(2x)-h(x)=3kx²/(2mg). Spring energy scales as x² while the frictional loss is unchanged.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Spring launch on a rough track: Answer all mandatory subparts. Show reasoning and calculations clearly. Use standard AP Physics notation and justify qualitative claims. A block of mass m is released from rest against a spring of constant k compressed a distance x. After losing contact with the spring, the block crosses a horizontal rough region of length L with kinetic-friction coefficient μ and then moves up a frictionless ramp. The zero of gravitational potential energy is the horizontal track. Express answers in terms of the given symbols and g."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-037", "content_item_id": "702fca06-c833-4262-a4ca-c1bf76252a84", "content_item_version_id": "96f0cca0-0b23-4d3d-91f1-d5c0bf65832c", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) For a static liquid, P=P_atm+ρgh, so the slope of P versus depth h is ρg. (b) Clamp the calibrated sensor in one orientation, measure depth vertically from the liquid surface, and record stable absolute-pressure readings at several depths over a broad range while keeping the liquid still and the sensor off the walls. (c) Plot P vertically against h horizontally and fit a line. Then ρ=slope/g and δρ=δslope/g. Compare the fitted intercept with the barometer's P_atm; disagreement beyond uncertainty indicates a sensor zero offset. (d) If every recorded depth has the same constant offset, only the fitted intercept changes; the slope and therefore the calculated density are unchanged.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) For a static liquid, P=P_atm+ρgh, so the slope of P versus depth h is ρg. (b) Clamp the calibrated sensor in one orientation, measure depth vertically from the liquid surface, and record stable absolute-pressure readings at several depths over a broad range while keeping the liquid still and the sensor off the walls. (c) Plot P vertically against h horizontally and fit a line. Then ρ=slope/g and δρ=δslope/g. Compare the fitted intercept with the barometer's P_atm; disagreement beyond uncertainty indicates a sensor zero offset. (d) If every recorded depth has the same constant offset, only the fitted intercept changes; the slope and therefore the calculated density are unchanged.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-02", "part-c-criterion-03", "part-c-criterion-04", "part-d-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-02"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 10, "criteria_covered": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-02", "part-c-criterion-03", "part-c-criterion-04", "part-d-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-02"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-038", "content_item_id": "10507b47-e925-46aa-a244-c72613b623c2", "content_item_version_id": "db659ebf-4ffa-4a30-aa77-f0fd3e2b1a3a", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) The total energy is E=(1/2)kA². At x=A/2, U=(1/2)k(A/2)²=E/4, so K=E-U=3E/4 and K>U. (b) For amplitude A, (1/2)kA²=(1/2)k(A/2)²+(1/2)mv1², giving v1=(A/2)sqrt(3k/m). For amplitude 2A at x=A, (1/2)k(2A)²=(1/2)kA²+(1/2)mv2², giving v2=A sqrt(3k/m)=2v1. (c) The claim is false: T=2πsqrt(m/k), independent of amplitude. Doubling amplitude doubles both travel distances and characteristic speeds, leaving the period unchanged.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) The total energy is E=(1/2)kA². At x=A/2, U=(1/2)k(A/2)²=E/4, so K=E-U=3E/4 and K>U. (b) For amplitude A, (1/2)kA²=(1/2)k(A/2)²+(1/2)mv1², giving v1=(A/2)sqrt(3k/m). For amplitude 2A at x=A, (1/2)k(2A)²=(1/2)kA²+(1/2)mv2², giving v2=A sqrt(3k/m)=2v1. (c) The claim is false: T=2πsqrt(m/k), independent of amplitude. Doubling amplitude doubles both travel distances and characteristic speeds, leaving the period unchanged.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-a-criterion-03", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-03", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-02"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 8, "criteria_covered": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-a-criterion-03", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-03", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-02"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "1", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-a-criterion-03", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-03", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-02"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) The total energy is E=(1/2)kA². At x=A/2, U=(1/2)k(A/2)²=E/4, so K=E-U=3E/4 and K>U. (b) For amplitude A, (1/2)kA²=(1/2)k(A/2)²+(1/2)mv1², giving v1=(A/2)sqrt(3k/m). For amplitude 2A at x=A, (1/2)k(2A)²=(1/2)kA²+(1/2)mv2², giving v2=A sqrt(3k/m)=2v1. (c) The claim is false: T=2πsqrt(m/k), independent of amplitude. Doubling amplitude doubles both travel distances and characteristic speeds, leaving the period unchanged.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Mass-spring amplitude and speed: Answer all mandatory subparts. Show reasoning and calculations clearly. Use standard AP Physics notation and justify qualitative claims. A cart of mass m attached to an ideal horizontal spring of constant k oscillates with amplitude A on a frictionless track. At one instant the cart is at x=A/2 and moving toward equilibrium. A second otherwise identical system has amplitude 2A."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-a-criterion-02", "part-a-criterion-03", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-02", "part-b-criterion-03", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-02"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) The total energy is E=(1/2)kA². At x=A/2, U=(1/2)k(A/2)²=E/4, so K=E-U=3E/4 and K>U. (b) For amplitude A, (1/2)kA²=(1/2)k(A/2)²+(1/2)mv1², giving v1=(A/2)sqrt(3k/m). For amplitude 2A at x=A, (1/2)k(2A)²=(1/2)kA²+(1/2)mv2², giving v2=A sqrt(3k/m)=2v1. (c) The claim is false: T=2πsqrt(m/k), independent of amplitude. Doubling amplitude doubles both travel distances and characteristic speeds, leaving the period unchanged.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Mass-spring amplitude and speed: Answer all mandatory subparts. 