ce_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the friction force acting on the crate and its resulting deceleration.\n\n(b) Predict how the deceleration changes if the coefficient of kinetic friction is doubled to 0.400, calculate the new deceleration, and explain why the deceleration is directly proportional to the coefficient of friction. A 5.00 kg crate slides across a horizontal floor with a coefficient of kinetic friction of 0.200 (g = 9.80 m/s^2). No other horizontal forces act once the crate is sliding."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-041", "content_item_id": "fc1ed479-20aa-4803-a110-eee861b5397a", "content_item_version_id": "f43cd467-46ad-4758-b6c9-21e3851a261e", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nI would vary climbing time or pace (independent variable) and compute average mechanical power P=mgh/t (dependent variable). I would keep the same climber, so mass m is fixed, and use the same staircase, so height h is fixed. Holding m and h constant isolates the effect of time on power.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "I would vary climbing time or pace (independent variable) and compute average mechanical power P=mgh/t (dependent variable). I would keep the same climber, so mass m is fixed, and use the same staircase, so height h is fixed. Holding m and h constant isolates the effect of time on power.", "criterion_keys": ["a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-control"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 3, "criteria_covered": ["a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-control"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-042", "content_item_id": "02b9352a-83e8-40d3-a8c7-07b58aa28ea1", "content_item_version_id": "2a7ee51f-c0d2-490c-bc20-2e749616868b", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) v=v0+at=-12.0+(4.00)(6.00)=+12.0 m/s. The displacement is Δx=v0t+(1/2)at²=(-12.0)(6.00)+(1/2)(4.00)(6.00)²=0 m. (b) The negative initial velocity means motion starts in the negative direction. Setting v=0 gives t=12.0/4.00=3.00 s; the car then reverses and moves in the positive direction.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) v=v0+at=-12.0+(4.00)(6.00)=+12.0 m/s. The displacement is Δx=v0t+(1/2)at²=(-12.0)(6.00)+(1/2)(4.00)(6.00)²=0 m. (b) The negative initial velocity means motion starts in the negative direction. Setting v=0 gives t=12.0/4.00=3.00 s; the car then reverses and moves in the positive direction.", "criterion_keys": ["a-displacement", "a-velocity", "b-explanation", "b-turnaround-time"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 4, "criteria_covered": ["a-displacement", "a-velocity", "b-explanation", "b-turnaround-time"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "12.0", "criterion_keys": ["a-displacement", "a-velocity", "b-explanation", "b-turnaround-time"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) v=v0+at=-12.0+(4.00)(6.00)=+12.0 m/s. The displacement is Δx=v0t+(1/2)at²=(-12.0)(6.00)+(1/2)(4.00)(6.00)²=0 m. (b) The negative initial velocity means motion starts in the negative direction. Setting v=0 gives t=12.0/4.00=3.00 s; the car then reverses and moves in the positive direction.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the car's velocity and net displacement during the 6.00 s interval.\n\n(b) Identify the time at which the car is momentarily at rest, and explain what the negative initial velocity together with this turnaround means about the car's direction of motion before and after that instant. A car moving along a straight road has an initial velocity of -12.0 m/s and a constant acceleration of +4.00 m/s^2 for 6.00 s."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "4.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-displacement", "a-velocity", "b-explanation", "b-turnaround-time"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) v=v0+at=-12.0+(4.00)(6.00)=+12.0 m/s. The displacement is Δx=v0t+(1/2)at²=(-12.0)(6.00)+(1/2)(4.00)(6.00)²=0 m. (b) The negative initial velocity means motion starts in the negative direction. Setting v=0 gives t=12.0/4.00=3.00 s; the car then reverses and moves in the positive direction.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the car's velocity and net displacement during the 6.00 s interval.\n\n(b) Identify the time at which the car is momentarily at rest, and explain what the negative initial velocity together with this turnaround means about the car's direction of motion before and after that instant. A car moving along a straight road has an initial velocity of -12.0 m/s and a constant acceleration of +4.00 m/s^2 for 6.00 s."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "6.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-displacement", "a-velocity", "b-explanation", "b-turnaround-time"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) v=v0+at=-12.0+(4.00)(6.00)=+12.0 m/s. The displacement is Δx=v0t+(1/2)at²=(-12.0)(6.00)+(1/2)(4.00)(6.00)²=0 m. (b) The negative initial velocity means motion starts in the negative direction. Setting v=0 gives t=12.0/