ement is Δx=v0t+(1/2)at²=(-12.0)(6.00)+(1/2)(4.00)(6.00)²=0 m. (b) The negative initial velocity means motion starts in the negative direction. Setting v=0 gives t=12.0/4.00=3.00 s; the car then reverses and moves in the positive direction.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the car's velocity and net displacement during the 6.00 s interval.\n\n(b) Identify the time at which the car is momentarily at rest, and explain what the negative initial velocity together with this turnaround means about the car's direction of motion before and after that instant. A car moving along a straight road has an initial velocity of -12.0 m/s and a constant acceleration of +4.00 m/s^2 for 6.00 s."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-043", "content_item_id": "e16deef1-cb7a-41a9-ad27-4559eb4056b0", "content_item_version_id": "0927826d-129f-490a-911c-ad68c38eb929", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nAlong the frictionless incline, ma=mg sin30.0°, so a=g sin30.0°=(9.80)(0.500)=4.90 m/s² down the incline. Perpendicular to it, N=mg cos30.0°=(4.00)(9.80)(0.866)=33.9 N. Mass cancels from ma=mg sinθ, so the acceleration is independent of mass.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "Along the frictionless incline, ma=mg sin30.0°, so a=g sin30.0°=(9.80)(0.500)=4.90 m/s² down the incline. Perpendicular to it, N=mg cos30.0°=(4.00)(9.80)(0.866)=33.9 N. Mass cancels from ma=mg sinθ, so the acceleration is independent of mass.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-accel", "part-a-normal", "part-b"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 3, "criteria_covered": ["part-a-accel", "part-a-normal", "part-b"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "0.0°", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-accel", "part-a-normal", "part-b"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "Along the frictionless incline, ma=mg sin30.0°, so a=g sin30.0°=(9.80)(0.500)=4.90 m/s² down the incline. Perpendicular to it, N=mg cos30.0°=(4.00)(9.80)(0.866)=33.9 N. Mass cancels from ma=mg sinθ, so the acceleration is independent of mass.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Derive expressions for the block's acceleration down the incline and the normal force on it, and evaluate their numeric values.\n\n(b) Explain why the acceleration does not depend on the block's mass. A 4.00 kg block slides down a frictionless incline angled at 30.0° above horizontal (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0.0°", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-accel", "part-a-normal", "part-b"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "Along the frictionless incline, ma=mg sin30.0°, so a=g sin30.0°=(9.80)(0.500)=4.90 m/s² down the incline. Perpendicular to it, N=mg cos30.0°=(4.00)(9.80)(0.866)=33.9 N. Mass cancels from ma=mg sinθ, so the acceleration is independent of mass.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Derive expressions for the block's acceleration down the incline and the normal force on it, and evaluate their numeric values.\n\n(b) Explain why the acceleration does not depend on the block's mass. A 4.00 kg block slides down a frictionless incline angled at 30.0° above horizontal (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "9.80", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-accel", "part-a-normal", "part-b"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "Along the frictionless incline, ma=mg sin30.0°, so a=g sin30.0°=(9.80)(0.500)=4.90 m/s² down the incline. Perpendicular to it, N=mg cos30.0°=(4.00)(9.80)(0.866)=33.9 N. Mass cancels from ma=mg sinθ, so the acceleration is independent of mass.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Derive expressions for the block's acceleration down the incline and the normal force on it, and evaluate their numeric values.\n\n(b) Explain why the acceleration does not depend on the block's mass. A 4.00 kg block slides down a frictionless incline angled at 30.0° above horizontal (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP