{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-044", "content_item_id": "ad105418-ee59-4654-afe4-2243b0171f6c", "content_item_version_id": "8a24e2ec-5604-4100-a3f9-565a0ed16d5b", "version_num": 1, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) Conservation of energy gives mgh=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2gh)=sqrt(2(9.80)(1.25))=4.95 m/s. (b) Doubling the height to 2.50 m gives v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.50))=7.00 m/s. Because v is proportional to sqrt(h), the speed increases by sqrt(2)≈1.41, not by 2; kinetic energy depends on v².", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) Conservation of energy gives mgh=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2gh)=sqrt(2(9.80)(1.25))=4.95 m/s. (b) Doubling the height to 2.50 m gives v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.50))=7.00 m/s. Because v is proportional to sqrt(h), the speed increases by sqrt(2)≈1.41, not by 2; kinetic energy depends on v².", "criterion_keys": ["a-original", "b-explanation", "b-new"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 3, "criteria_covered": ["a-original", "b-explanation", "b-new"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "1", "criterion_keys": ["a-original", "b-explanation", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Conservation of energy gives mgh=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2gh)=sqrt(2(9.80)(1.25))=4.95 m/s. (b) Doubling the height to 2.50 m gives v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.50))=7.00 m/s. Because v is proportional to sqrt(h), the speed increases by sqrt(2)≈1.41, not by 2; kinetic energy depends on v².", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the ramp using conservation of energy.\n\n(b) Predict how the speed at the bottom changes if the height is doubled to 2.50 m, calculate the new speed, and explain why the speed does not simply double. A 2.00 kg block starts from rest and slides down a frictionless ramp from a height of 1.25 m (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-original", "b-explanation", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Conservation of energy gives mgh=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2gh)=sqrt(2(9.80)(1.25))=4.95 m/s. (b) Doubling the height to 2.50 m gives v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.50))=7.00 m/s. Because v is proportional to sqrt(h), the speed increases by sqrt(2)≈1.41, not by 2; kinetic energy depends on v².", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the ramp using conservation of energy.\n\n(b) Predict how the speed at the bottom changes if the height is doubled to 2.50 m, calculate the new speed, and explain why the speed does not simply double. A 2.00 kg block starts from rest and slides down a frictionless ramp from a height of 1.25 m (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-original", "b-explanation", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Conservation of energy gives mgh=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2gh)=sqrt(2(9.80)(1.25))=4.95 m/s. (b) Doubling the height to 2.50 m gives v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.50))=7.00 m/s. Because v is proportional to sqrt(h), the speed increases by sqrt(2)≈1.41, not by 2; kinetic energy depends on v².", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the ramp using conservation of energy.\n\n(b) Predict how the speed at the bottom changes if the height is doubled to 2.50 m, calculate the new speed, and explain why the speed does not simply double. A 2.00 kg block starts from rest and slides down a frictionless ramp from a height of 1.25 m (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "9.80", "criterion_keys": ["a-original", "b-explanation", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Conservation of energy gives mgh=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2gh)=sqrt(2(9.80)(1.25))=4.95 m/s. (b) Doubling the height to 2.50 m gives v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.50))=7.00 m/s. Because v is proportional to sqrt(h), the speed increases by sqrt(2)≈1.41, not by 2; kinetic energy depends on v².", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the ramp using conservation of energy.\n\n(b) Predict how the speed at the bottom changes if the height is doubled to 2.50 m, calculate the new speed, and explain why the speed does not simply double. A 2.00 kg block starts from rest and slides down a frictionless ramp from a height of 1.25 m (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1.25", "criterion_keys": ["a-original", "b-explanation", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Conservation of energy gives mgh=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2gh)=sqrt(2(9.80)(1.25))=4.95 m/s. (b) Doubling the height to 2.50 m gives v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.50))=7.00 m/s. Because v is proportional to sqrt(h), the speed increases by sqrt(2)≈1.41, not by 2; kinetic energy depends on v².", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the