{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-045", "content_item_id": "6f76e68b-1534-4c5b-a620-178b21990107", "content_item_version_id": "408b1a87-6b9a-438d-93e8-9881ea534971", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nThe independent variable is launch angle and the dependent variable is horizontal range. Keep launch speed fixed (and also keep launch and landing heights and the ball/launcher fixed). Changing speed would confound the angle comparison because, for negligible drag and equal launch/landing heights, R=v²sin(2θ)/g depends on both v and θ.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "The independent variable is launch angle and the dependent variable is horizontal range. Keep launch speed fixed (and also keep launch and landing heights and the ball/launcher fixed). Changing speed would confound the angle comparison because, for negligible drag and equal launch/landing heights, R=v²sin(2θ)/g depends on both v and θ.", "criterion_keys": ["a-control", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-justification"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 4, "criteria_covered": ["a-control", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-justification"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-control", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-justification"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "The independent variable is launch angle and the dependent variable is horizontal range. Keep launch speed fixed (and also keep launch and landing heights and the ball/launcher fixed). Changing speed would confound the angle comparison because, for negligible drag and equal launch/landing heights, R=v²sin(2θ)/g depends on both v and θ.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design the experiment: identify the independent variable, the dependent variable, and one variable to hold constant as a control.\n\n(b) Explain why holding the launch speed constant across trials is necessary to isolate the effect of angle on range. A student launches a ball from a spring launcher at various angles to study how launch angle affects horizontal range."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-046", "content_item_id": "dcc448ec-14be-40fd-a31d-6efb71d72d0d", "content_item_version_id": "f0613586-dd55-4891-b441-ce9abccc3b28", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nFor the upper block to move with the lower one, static friction must provide ma. Its maximum is f_s,max=μ_smg, so a_max=f_s,max/m=μ_sg=(0.250)(9.80)=2.45 m/s². Above this acceleration the required friction exceeds its maximum, so the upper block cannot keep pace and slides backward relative to the lower block; no kinetic-friction value is assumed.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "For the upper block to move with the lower one, static friction must provide ma. Its maximum is f_s,max=μ_smg, so a_max=f_s,max/m=μ_sg=(0.250)(9.80)=2.45 m/s². Above this acceleration the required friction exceeds its maximum, so the upper block cannot keep pace and slides backward relative to the lower block; no kinetic-friction value is assumed.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 2, "criteria_covered": ["part-a", "part-b"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "0.250", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "For the upper block to move with the lower one, static friction must provide ma. Its maximum is f_s,max=μ_smg, so a_max=f_s,max/m=μ_sg=(0.250)(9.80)=2.45 m/s². Above this acceleration the required friction exceeds its maximum, so the upper block cannot keep pace and slides backward relative to the lower block; no kinetic-friction value is assumed.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the maximum acceleration of the two-block system before the top block begins to slip.\n\n(b) Explain, using Newton's second law applied to the top block, what happens as the applied force on the lower block is increased beyond the level that produces this maximum acceleration. A 2.00 kg block rests on top of a 6.00 kg block on a frictionless table; the coefficient of static friction between the blocks is 0.250 (g = 9.80 m/s^2). A horizontal force is applied to the lower block."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "9.80", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "For the upper block to move with the lower one, static friction must provide ma. Its maximum is f_s,max=μ_smg, so a_max=f_s,max/m=μ_sg=(0.250)(9.80)=2.45 m/s². Above this acceleration the required friction exceeds its maximum, so the upper block cannot keep pace and slides backward relative to the lower block; no kinetic-friction value is assumed.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the maximum acceleration of the two-block system before the top block begins to slip.\n\n(b) Explain, using Newton's second law applied to the top block, what happens as the applied force on the lower block is increased beyond the level that produces this maximum acceleration. A 2.00 kg block rests on top of a 6.00 kg block on a frictionless table; the coefficient of static friction between the blocks is 0.250 (g = 9.80 m/s^2). A horizontal force is applied to the lower block."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2.45", "criterion_keys": ["part-a", "part-b"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "For the upper block to move with the lower one, static friction must provide ma. Its maximum is f_s,max=μ_smg, so a_max=f_s,max/m=μ_sg=(0.250)(9.80)=2.45 m/s². Above this acceleration the required friction exceeds its maximum, so the upper block cannot keep pace and slides backward relative to the lower block; no kinetic-friction value is assumed.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the maximum acceleration of the two-block system before the top block begins to slip.\n\n(b) Explain, using Newton's second law applied to the top block, what happens as the applied force on the lower block is increased beyond the level that produces this maximum acceleration. A 2.00 kg block rests on top of a 6.00 kg block on a frictionless table; the coefficient of static friction between the blocks is 0.250 (g = 9.80 m/s^2). A horizontal force is applied to the lower block."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-047", "content_item_id": "4256c29a-928e-45e8-a1cb-a457e6aa51ff", "content_item_version_id": "050bf660-8498-452f-bfad-e521280c3e46", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nThe acceleration is a=(8.00-0)/5.00=1.60 m/s², so the constant net force is F=ma=(70.0)(1.60)=112 N. The work is ΔK=(1/2)(70.0)(8.00²)=2240 J. Average power is W/Δt=2240/5.00=448 W. At 5.00 s, instantaneous power is P=Fv=(112)(8.00)=896 W, larger than the average because constant force and linearly increasing speed make power grow linearly from zero.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "The acceleration is a=(8.00-0)/5.00=1.60 m/s², so the constant net force is F=ma=(70.0)(1.60)=112 N. The work is ΔK=(1/2)(70.0)(8.00²)=2240 J. Average power is W/Δt=2240/5.00=448 W. At 5.00 s, instantaneous power is P=Fv=(112)(8.00)=896 W, larger than the average because constant force and linearly increasing speed make power grow linearly from zero.", "criterion_keys": ["a-power", "a-work", "b-explanation"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 3, "criteria_covered": ["a-power", "a-work", "b-explanation"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "8.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-power", "a-work", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "The acceleration is a=(8.00-0)/5.00=1.60 m/s², so the constant net force is F=ma=(70.0)(1.60)=112 N. The work is ΔK=(1/2)(70.0)(8.00²)=2240 J. Average power is W/Δt=2240/5.00=448 W. At 5.00 s, instantaneous power is P=Fv=(112)(8.00)=896 W, larger than the average because constant force and linearly increasing speed make power grow linearly from zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the work done on the cyclist and the average power output over this interval.\n\n(b) Explain why the average power is less than the instantaneous power output at t = 5.00 s. A 70.0 kg cyclist, accelerating uniformly (constant acceleration), accelerates from rest to 8.00 m/s in 5.00 s on level ground; assume negligible resistive forces."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0", "criterion_keys": ["a-power", "a-work", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "The acceleration is a=(8.00-0)/5.00=1.60 m/s², so the constant net force is F=ma=(70.0)(1.60)=112 N. The work is ΔK=(1/2)(70.0)(8.00²)=2240 J. Average power is W/Δt=2240/5.00=448 W. At 5.00 s, instantaneous power is P=Fv=(112)(8.00)=896 W, larger than the average because constant force and linearly increasing speed make power grow linearly from zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the work done on the cyclist and the average power output over this interval.\n\n(b) Explain why the average power is less than the instantaneous power output at t = 5.00 s. A 70.0 kg cyclist, accelerating uniformly (constant acceleration), accelerates from rest to 8.00 m/s in 5.00 s on level ground; assume negligible resistive forces."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "5.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-power", "a-work", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "The acceleration is a=(8.00-0)/5.00=1.60 m/s², so the constant net force is F=ma=(70.0)(1.60)=112 N. The work is ΔK=(1/2)(70.0)(8.00²)=2240 J. Average power is W/Δt=2240/5.00=448 W. At 5.00 s, instantaneous power is P=Fv=(112)(8.00)=896 W, larger than the average because constant force and linearly increasing speed make power grow linearly from zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the work done on the cyclist and the average power output over this interval.\n\n(b) Explain why the average power is less than the instantaneous power output at t = 5.00 s. A 70.0 kg cyclist, accelerating uniformly (constant acceleration), accelerates from rest to 8.00 m/s in 5.00 s on level ground; assume negligible resistive forces."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1.60", "criterion_keys": ["a-power", "a-work", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "The acceleration is a=(8.00-0)/5.00=1.60 m/s², so the constant net force is F=ma=(70.0)(1.60)=112 N. The work is ΔK=(1/2)(70.0)(8.00²)=2240 J. Average power is W/Δt=2240/5.00=448 W. At 5.00 s, instantaneous power is P=Fv=(112)(8.00)=896 W, larger than the average because constant force and linearly increasing speed make power grow linearly from zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the work done on the cyclist and the average power output over this interval.\n\n(b) Explain why the average power is less than the instantaneous power output at t = 5.00 s. A 70.0 kg cyclist, accelerating uniformly (constant acceleration), accelerates from rest to 8.00 m/s in 5.00 s on level ground; assume negligible resistive forces."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "70.0", "criterion_keys": ["a-power", "a-work", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "The acceleration is a=(8.00-0)/5.00=1.60 m/s², so the constant net force is F=ma=(70.0)(1.60)=112 N. The work is ΔK=(1/2)(70.0)(8.00²)=2240 J. Average power is W/Δt=2240/5.00=448 W. At 5.00 s, instantaneous power is P=Fv=(112)(8.00)=896 W, larger than the average because constant force and linearly increasing speed make power grow linearly from zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the work done on the cyclist and the average power output over this interval.\n\n(b) Explain why the average power is less than the instantaneous power output at t = 5.00 s. A 70.0 kg cyclist, accelerating uniformly (constant acceleration), accelerates from rest to 8.00 m/s in 5.00 s on level ground; assume negligible resistive forces."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1.60", "criterion_keys": ["a-power", "a-work", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "The acceleration is a=(8.00-0)/5.00=1.60 m/s², so the constant net force is F=ma=(70.0)(1.60)=112 N. The work is ΔK=(1/2)(70.0)(8.00²)=2240 J. Average power is W/Δt=2240/5.00=448 W. At 5.00 s, instantaneous power is P=Fv=(112)(8.00)=896 W, larger than the average because constant force and linearly increasing speed make power grow linearly from zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the work done on the cyclist and the average power output over this interval.\n\n(b) Explain why the average power is less than the instantaneous power output at t = 5.00 s. A 70.0 kg cyclist, accelerating uniformly (constant acceleration), accelerates from rest to 8.00 m/s in 5.00 s on level ground; assume negligible resistive forces."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSIC