{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-048", "content_item_id": "7b739aba-0777-4293-a51f-4ad2b8da70b5", "content_item_version_id": "2a52b9bb-636d-43f7-8ffe-d67a746e0bfb", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) The closing speed is 28.0-20.0=8.00 m/s, so t=300/8.00=37.5 s. (b) If the closing speed becomes 12.0 m/s, t=300/12.0=25.0 s. In general t=Δx/(v_B-v_A), so catch-up time decreases inversely as closing speed increases.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) The closing speed is 28.0-20.0=8.00 m/s, so t=300/8.00=37.5 s. (b) If the closing speed becomes 12.0 m/s, t=300/12.0=25.0 s. In general t=Δx/(v_B-v_A), so catch-up time decreases inversely as closing speed increases.", "criterion_keys": ["a-catchup", "b-combined"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 2, "criteria_covered": ["a-catchup", "b-combined"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "28.0", "criterion_keys": ["a-catchup", "b-combined"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) The closing speed is 28.0-20.0=8.00 m/s, so t=300/8.00=37.5 s. (b) If the closing speed becomes 12.0 m/s, t=300/12.0=25.0 s. In general t=Δx/(v_B-v_A), so catch-up time decreases inversely as closing speed increases.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the time for car B to catch up to car A.\n\n(b) Predict how the catch-up time changes if car B's speed increases to 32.0 m/s, calculate the new catch-up time, and explain the relationship between closing speed and catch-up time. Car A travels at a constant 20.0 m/s eastward. Car B, 300 m behind car A on the same road, travels at a constant 28.0 m/s eastward, both starting at t=0. Treat both cars as point objects traveling in the same lane."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "20.0", "criterion_keys": ["a-catchup", "b-combined"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) The closing speed is 28.0-20.0=8.00 m/s, so t=300/8.00=37.5 s. (b) If the closing speed becomes 12.0 m/s, t=300/12.0=25.0 s. In general t=Δx/(v_B-v_A), so catch-up time decreases inversely as closing speed increases.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the time for car B to catch up to car A.\n\n(b) Predict how the catch-up time changes if car B's speed increases to 32.0 m/s, calculate the new catch-up time, and explain the relationship between closing speed and catch-up time. Car A travels at a constant 20.0 m/s eastward. Car B, 300 m behind car A on the same road, travels at a constant 28.0 m/s eastward, both starting at t=0. Treat both cars as point objects traveling in the same lane."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "8.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-catchup", "b-combined"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) The closing speed is 28.0-20.0=8.00 m/s, so t=300/8.00=37.5 s. (b) If the closing speed becomes 12.0 m/s, t=300/12.0=25.0 s. In general t=Δx/(v_B-v_A), so catch-up time decreases inversely as closing speed increases.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the time for car B to catch up to car A.\n\n(b) Predict how the catch-up time changes if car B's speed increases to 32.0 m/s, calculate the new catch-up time, and explain the relationship between closing speed and catch-up time. Car A travels at a constant 20.0 m/s eastward. Car B, 300 m behind car A on the same road, travels at a constant 28.0 m/s eastward, both starting at t=0. Treat both cars as point objects traveling in the same lane."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "300", "criterion_keys": ["a-catchup", "b-combined"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) The closing speed is 28.0-20.0=8.00 m/s, so t=300/8.00=37.5 s. (b) If the closing speed becomes 12.0 m/s, t=300/12.0=25.0 s. In general t=Δx/(v_B-v_A), so catch-up time decreases inversely as closing speed increases.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the time for car B to catch up to car A.\n\n(b) Predict how the catch-up time changes if car B's speed increases to 32.0 m/s, calculate the new catch-up time, and explain the relationship between closing speed and catch-up time. Car A travels at a constant 20.0 m/s eastward. Car B, 300 m behind car A on the same road, travels at a constant 28.0 m/s eastward, both starting at t=0. Treat both cars as point objects traveling in the same lane."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "8.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-catchup", "b-combined"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) The closing speed is 28.0-20.0=8.00 m/s, so t=300/8.00=37.5 s. (b) If the closing speed becomes 12.0 m/s, t=300/12.0=25.0 s. In general t=Δx/(v_B-v_A), so catch-up time decreases inversely as closing speed increases.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the time for car B to catch up to car A.\n\n(b) Predict how the catch-up time changes if car B's speed increases to 32.0 m/s, calcula