creases.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the time for car B to catch up to car A.\n\n(b) Predict how the catch-up time changes if car B's speed increases to 32.0 m/s, calculate the new catch-up time, and explain the relationship between closing speed and catch-up time. Car A travels at a constant 20.0 m/s eastward. Car B, 300 m behind car A on the same road, travels at a constant 28.0 m/s eastward, both starting at t=0. Treat both cars as point objects traveling in the same lane."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "25.0", "criterion_keys": ["a-catchup", "b-combined"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) The closing speed is 28.0-20.0=8.00 m/s, so t=300/8.00=37.5 s. (b) If the closing speed becomes 12.0 m/s, t=300/12.0=25.0 s. In general t=Δx/(v_B-v_A), so catch-up time decreases inversely as closing speed increases.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the time for car B to catch up to car A.\n\n(b) Predict how the catch-up time changes if car B's speed increases to 32.0 m/s, calculate the new catch-up time, and explain the relationship between closing speed and catch-up time. Car A travels at a constant 20.0 m/s eastward. Car B, 300 m behind car A on the same road, travels at a constant 28.0 m/s eastward, both starting at t=0. Treat both cars as point objects traveling in the same lane."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-049", "content_item_id": "daf8dc45-7984-4ae5-ab97-593e2f79d678", "content_item_version_id": "76d2506f-9aeb-4d39-b20d-804df229efd5", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nUse the same spring with the same unstretched length and orientation, and keep the measurement method and temperature fixed. Vary hanging mass/applied weight (independent variable) and measure extension x after equilibrium (dependent variable). Plot applied force F=mg vertically versus extension x horizontally; in the Hookean region the graph is linear with slope k. Data beyond the elastic region must be excluded because F is no longer proportional to x.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "Use the same spring with the same unstretched length and orientation, and keep the measurement method and temperature fixed. Vary hanging mass/applied weight (independent variable) and measure extension x after equilibrium (dependent variable). Plot applied force F=mg vertically versus extension x horizontally; in the Hookean region the graph is linear with slope k. Data beyond the elastic region must be excluded because F is no longer proportional to x.", "criterion_keys": ["a-control1", "a-control2", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-graph", "b-hookean"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 6, "criteria_covered": ["a-control1", "a-control2", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-graph", "b-hookean"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [], "confidence": "medium", "flags": ["textual_graph_or_diagram_representation", "cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-050", "content_item_id": "f42b6500-d7a7-4689-a87d-1de3c580cea5", "content_item_version_id": "78097e73-05c4-4498-8407-f9fbc9060755", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nInitial gravitational energy is U_i=mgh=(400)(9.80)(20.0)=78,400 J. Final kinetic energy is K_f=(1/2)mv²=(1/2)(400)(18.0²)=64,800 J. Thus W_nc=E_f-E_i=64,800-78,400=-13,600 J. The negative sign means friction and air resistance removed mechanical energy and converted it to thermal or other nonmechanical energy.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "Initial gravitational energy is U_i=mgh=(400)(9.80)(20.0)=78,400 J. Final kinetic energy is K_f=(1/2)mv²=(1/2)(400)(18.0²)=64,800 J. Thus W_nc=E_f-E_i=64,800-78,400=-13,600 J. The negative sign means friction and air resistance removed mechanical energy and converted it to thermal or other nonmechanical energy.", "criterion_keys": ["a-KE", "a-PE", "a-Wnc", "b-explanation"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 4, "criteria_covered": ["a-KE", "a-PE", "a-Wnc", "b-explanation"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "400", "criterion_keys": ["a-KE", "a-PE", "a-Wnc", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "Initial gravitational energy is U_i=mgh=(400)(9.80)(20.0)=78,400 J. Final kinetic energy is K_f=(1/2)mv²=(1/2)(400)(18.0²)=64,800 J. Thus W_nc=E_f-E_i=64,800-78,400=-13,600 J. The negative sign means friction and air resistance removed mechanical energy and converted it to thermal or other nonmechanical energy.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the car's initial potential energy, final kinetic energy, and the total nonconservative work done on the car during the descent.\n\n(b) Explain the physical meaning of the sign of this nonconservative work and what it implies about the car's mechanical energy. A 400 kg roller-coaster car starts from rest at the top of a 20.0 m hill and reaches a speed of 18.0 m/s at the bottom, where friction and air resistance act on the car (g = 9.80 m/s^2). Define the bottom of the hill as the zero level for gravitational potential energy."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "9.80", "criterion_keys": ["a-KE", "a-PE", "a-Wnc", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "Initial gravitational energy is U_i=mgh=(400)(9.80)(20.0)=78,400 J. Final kinetic energy is K_f=(1/2)mv²=(1/2)(400)(18.0²)=64,800 J. Thus W_nc=E_f-E_i=64,800-78,400=-13,600 J. The negative sign means friction and air resistance removed mechanical energy and converted it to thermal or other nonmechanical energy.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the car's initial potential energy, final kinetic energy, and the total nonconservative work done on the car during the descent.\n\n(b) Explain the physical meaning of the sign of this nonconservative work and what it implies about the car's mechanical energy. A 400 kg roller-coaster car starts from rest at the top of a 20.0 m hill and reaches a speed of 18.0 m/s at the bottom, where friction and air resistance act on the car (g = 9.80 m/s^2). Define the bottom of the hill as the zero level for gravitational potential energy."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "20.0", "criterion_keys": ["a-KE", "a-PE", "a-Wnc", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "Initial gravitational energy is U_i=mgh=(400)