ion.\n\n(a) Calculate the car's initial potential energy, final kinetic energy, and the total nonconservative work done on the car during the descent.\n\n(b) Explain the physical meaning of the sign of this nonconservative work and what it implies about the car's mechanical energy. A 400 kg roller-coaster car starts from rest at the top of a 20.0 m hill and reaches a speed of 18.0 m/s at the bottom, where friction and air resistance act on the car (g = 9.80 m/s^2). Define the bottom of the hill as the zero level for gravitational potential energy."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "400", "criterion_keys": ["a-KE", "a-PE", "a-Wnc", "b-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "Initial gravitational energy is U_i=mgh=(400)(9.80)(20.0)=78,400 J. Final kinetic energy is K_f=(1/2)mv²=(1/2)(400)(18.0²)=64,800 J. Thus W_nc=E_f-E_i=64,800-78,400=-13,600 J. The negative sign means friction and air resistance removed mechanical energy and converted it to thermal or other nonmechanical energy.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the car's initial potential energy, final kinetic energy, and the total nonconservative work done on the car during the descent.\n\n(b) Explain the physical meaning of the sign of this nonconservative work and what it implies about the car's mechanical energy. A 400 kg roller-coaster car starts from rest at the top of a 20.0 m hill and reaches a speed of 18.0 m/s at the bottom, where friction and air resistance act on the car (g = 9.80 m/s^2). Define the bottom of the hill as the zero level for gravitational potential energy."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "18.0", "criterion_keys": ["a-KE"