)=3.00(9.80+2.45)=36.8 N. (b) With m2=7.00 kg, a=(7.00-3.00)9.80/(10.00)=3.92 m/s². The imbalance doubles from 2.00 to 4.00 kg while total mass grows only from 8.00 to 10.00 kg, factors 2 and 1.25, so acceleration increases. As m2 approaches infinity with m1 fixed, (m2-m1)/(m2+m1) approaches 1 and a approaches g.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Draw free-body diagrams for both blocks, write Newton's second law for each block, state the modeling assumptions used, and calculate the acceleration of the system and the tension in the string.\n\n(b) Predict how the acceleration changes if the 5.00 kg mass is increased to 7.00 kg (the 3.00 kg mass unchanged), calculate the new acceleration, and explain the trend by comparing how the mass imbalance and the total mass each change. An Atwood machine has masses of 3.00 kg and 5.00 kg connected by a light, inextensible string over a frictionless, massless pulley (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "4.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-modeling", "a-newton", "a-tension", "b-explanation", "b-limit", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) Each block has weight downward and tension upward. For the 5.00 kg descending block, m2g-T=m2a; for the 3.00 kg ascending block, T-m1g=m1a. The light inextensible string and massless frictionless pulley give equal tension and equal acceleration magnitude. Adding equations gives a=(m2-m1)g/(m1+m2)=(2.00)(9.80)/8.00=2.45 m/s², and T=m1(g+a)=3.00(9.80+2.45)=36.8 N. (b) With m2=7.00 kg, a=(7.00-3.00)9.80/(10.00)=3.92 m/s². The imbalance doubles from 2.00 to 4.00 kg while total mass grows only from 8.00 to 10.00 kg, factors 2 and 1.25, so acceleration increases. As m2 approaches infinity with m1 fixed, (m2-m1)/(m2+m1) approaches 1 and a approaches g.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Draw free-body diagrams for both blocks, write Newton's second law for each block, state the modeling assumptions used, and calculate the acceleration of the system and the tension in the string.\n\n(b) Predict how the acceleration changes if the 5.00 kg mass is increased to 7.00 kg (the 3.00 kg mass unchanged), calculate the new acceleration, and explain the trend by comparing how the mass imbalance and the total mass each change. An Atwood machine has masses of 3.00 kg and 5.00 kg connected by a light, inextensible string over a frictionless, massless pulley (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "8.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-modeling", "a-newton", "a-tension", "b-explanation", "b-limit", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) Each block has weight downward and tension upward. For the 5.00 kg descending block, m2g-T=m2a; for the 3.00 kg ascending block, T-m1g=m1a. The light inextensible string and massless frictionless pulley give equal tension and equal acceleration magnitude. Adding equations gives a=(m2-m1)g/(m1+m2)=(2.00)(9.80)/8.00=2.45 m/s², and T=m1(g+a)=3.00(9.80+2.45)=36.8 N. (b) With m2=7.00 kg, a=(7.00-3.00)9.80/(10.00)=3.92 m/s². The imbalance doubles from 2.00 to 4.00 kg while total mass grows only from 8.00 to 10.00 kg, factors 2 and 1.25, so acceleration increases. As m2 approaches infinity with m1 fixed, (m2-m1)/(m2+m1) approaches 1 and a approaches g.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Draw free-body diagrams for both blocks, write Newton's second law for each block, state the modeling assumptio