alance and the total mass each change. An Atwood machine has masses of 3.00 kg and 5.00 kg connected by a light, inextensible string over a frictionless, massless pulley (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-modeling", "a-newton", "a-tension", "b-explanation", "b-limit", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Each block has weight downward and tension upward. For the 5.00 kg descending block, m2g-T=m2a; for the 3.00 kg ascending block, T-m1g=m1a. The light inextensible string and massless frictionless pulley give equal tension and equal acceleration magnitude. Adding equations gives a=(m2-m1)g/(m1+m2)=(2.00)(9.80)/8.00=2.45 m/s², and T=m1(g+a)=3.00(9.80+2.45)=36.8 N. (b) With m2=7.00 kg, a=(7.00-3.00)9.80/(10.00)=3.92 m/s². The imbalance doubles from 2.00 to 4.00 kg while total mass grows only from 8.00 to 10.00 kg, factors 2 and 1.25, so acceleration increases. As m2 approaches infinity with m1 fixed, (m2-m1)/(m2+m1) approaches 1 and a approaches g.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Draw free-body diagrams for both blocks, write Newton's second law for each block, state the modeling assumptions used, and calculate the acceleration of the system and the tension in the string.\n\n(b) Predict how the acceleration changes if the 5.00 kg mass is increased to 7.00 kg (the 3.00 kg mass unchanged), calculate the new acceleration, and explain the trend by comparing how the mass imbalance and the total mass each change. An Atwood machine has masses of 3.00 kg and 5.00 kg connected by a light, inextensible string over a frictionless, massless pulley (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1.25", "criterion_keys": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-modeling", "a-newton", "a-tension", "b-explanation", "b-limit", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) Each block has weight downward and tension upward. For the 5.00 kg descending block, m2g-T=m2a; for the 3.00 kg ascending block, T-m1g=m1a. The light inextensible string and massless frictionless pulley give equal tension and equal acceleration magnitude. Adding equations gives a=(m2-m1)g/(m1+m2)=(2.00)(9.80)/8.00=2.45 m/s², and T=m1(g+a)=3.00(9.80+2.45)=36.8 N. (b) With m2=7.00 kg, a=(7.00-3.00)9.80/(10.00)=3.92 m/s². The imbalance doubles from 2.00 to 4.00 kg while total mass grows only from 8.00 to 10.00 kg, factors 2 and 1.25, so acceleration increases. As m2 approaches infinity with m1 fixed, (m2-m1)/(m2+m1) approaches 1 and a approaches g.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Draw free-body diagrams for both blocks, write Newton's second law for each block, state the modeling assumptions used, and calculate the acceleration of the system and the tension in the string.\n\n(b) Predict how the acceleration changes if the 5.00 kg mass is increased to 7.00 kg (the 3.00 kg mass unchanged), calculate the new acceleration, and explain the trend by comparing how the mass imbalance and the total mass each change. An Atwood machine has masses of 3.00 kg and 5.00 kg connected by a light, inextensible string over a frictionless, massless pulley (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1", "criterion_keys": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-modeling", "a-newton", "a-tension", "b-explanation", "b-limit", "b-new"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) Each block has weight downward and tension upward. For the 5.00 kg descending block, m2g-T=m2a; for the 3.00 kg ascending block, T-m1g=m1a. The light inextensible string and massless frictionless pulley give equal tension and equal acceleration magnitude. Adding equations gives a=(m2-m1)g/(m1+m2)=(2.00)(9.80)/8.00=2.45 m/s², and T=m1(g+a)=3.00(9.80+2.45)=36.8 N. (b) With m2=7.00 kg, a=(7.00-3.00)9.80/(10.00)=3.92 m/s². The imbalance doubles from 2.00 to 4.00 kg while total mass grows only from 8.00 to 10.00 kg, factors 2 and 1.25, so acceleration increases. As m2 approaches infinity with m1 fixed, (m2-m1)/(m2+m1) approaches 1 and a approaches g.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Draw free-body diagrams for both blocks, write Newton's second law for each block, state the modeling assumptions used, and calculate the acceleration of the system and the tension in the string.\n\n(b) Predict how the acceleration changes if the 5.00 kg mass is increased to 7.00 kg (the 3.00 kg mass unchanged), calculate the new acceleration, and explain the trend by comparing how the mass imbalance and the total mass each change. An Atwood machine has masses of 3.00 kg and 5.00 kg connected by a light, inextensible string over a frictionless, massless pulley (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "medium", "flags": ["textual_graph_or_diagram_representation", "cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-053", "content_item_id": "82a7f071-ca1e-4260-a61b-62411cd4d79d", "content_item_version_id": "dc25829e-0b21-4dae-9e99-dbd3f5201651", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\nKeep the same block, surface material/condition, and level track. Vary initial speed v0 (independent variable) and measure stopping distance d (dependent variable). Work-energy gives (1/2)mv0²=μkmgd, so μk=v0²/(2gd). The mass cancels because both initial kinetic energy and friction work are proportional to m.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "Keep the same block, surface material/condition, and level track. Vary initial speed v0 (independent variable) and measure stopping distance d (dependent variable). Work-energy gives (1/2)mv0²=μkmgd, so μk=v0²/(2gd). The mass cancels because both initial kinetic energy and friction work are proportional to m.", "criterion_keys": ["a-control", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-derivation", "b-massexplanation"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 5, "criteria_covered": ["a-control", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-derivation", "b-massexplanation"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "1", "criterion_keys": ["a-control", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-derivation", "b-massexplanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "Keep the same block, surface material/condition, and level track. Vary initial speed v0 (independent variable) and measure stopping distance d (dependent variable). Work-energy gives (1/2)mv0²=μkmgd, so μk=v0²/(2gd). The mass cancels because both initial kinetic energy and friction work are proportional to m.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design the experiment: identify the independent variable, the dependent variable, and one variable to hold constant as a control.\n\n(b) Derive an expression for the coefficient of kinetic friction in terms of the initial speed and stopping distance using the work-energy theorem, and explain why the block's mass does not need to be known. A student wants to determine the coefficient of kinetic friction between a block and a horizontal surface by giving the block an initial push and measuring how far it slides before stopping. The push ends before the block begins the measured sliding interval, and the coefficient of kinetic friction remains constant during the slide."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-control", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-derivation", "b-massexplanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "Keep the same block, surface material/condition, and level track. Vary initial speed v0 (independent variable) and measure stopping distance d (dependent variable). Work-energy gives (1/2)mv0²=μkmgd, so μk=v0²/(2gd). The mass cancels because both initial kinetic energy and friction work are proportional to m.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design the experiment: identify the independent variable, the dependent variable, and one variable to hold constant as a control.\n\n(b) Derive an expression for the coefficient of kinetic friction in terms of the initial speed and stopping distance using the work-energy theorem, and explain why the block's mass does not need to be known. A student wants to determine the coefficient of kinetic friction between a block and a horizontal surface by giving the block an initial push and measuring how far it slides before stopping. The push ends before the block begins the measured sliding interval, and the coefficient of kinetic friction remains constant during the slide."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-054", "content_item_id": "73ed86fc-fcd5-4834-a409-9c15eaa2cce3", "content_item_version_id": "4c283652-3ddd-4d93-99f7-186acbed682d", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) v0x=25.0 cos40.0° and v0y=25.0 sin40.0°. The maximum height is h=v0y²/(2g)=13.2 m, and flight time is t=2v0y/g=3.28 s. Combining R=v0x t with t=2v0y/g gives R=2v0xv0y/g=v0²sin(2θ)/g; thus R=(25.0²)sin80.0°/9.80=62.8 m. (b) At 50.0°, R=(25.0²)sin100°/9.80=62.8 m, exactly equal to the 40.0° range because 40° and 50° are complementary, their doubled angles are supplementary, and sin(180°-x)=sin x.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) v0x=25.0 cos40.0° and v0y=25.0 sin40.0°. The maximum height is h=v0y²/(2g)=13.2 m, and flight time is t=2v0y/g=3.28 s. Combining R=v0x t with t=2v0y/g gives R=2v0xv0y/g=v0²sin(2θ)/g; thus R=(25.0²)sin80.0°/9.80=62.8 m. (b) At 50.0°, R=(25.0²)sin100°/9.80=62.8 m, exactly equal to the 40.0° range because 40° and 50° are complementary, their doubled angles are supplementary, and sin(180°-x)=sin x.", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-range", "a-symbolic-range", "a-time", "b-50deg-range", "b-symmetry-explanation"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 6, "criteria_covered": ["a-height", "a-range", "a-symbolic-range", "a-time", "b-50deg-range", "b-symmetry-explanation"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "25.0", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-range", "a-symbolic-range", "a-time", "b-50deg-range", "b-symmetry-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) v0x=25.0 cos40.0° and v0y=25.0 sin40.0°. The maximum height is h=v0y²/(2g)=13.2 m, and flight time is t=2v0y/g=3.28 s. Combining R=v0x t with t=2v0y/g gives R=2v0xv0y/g=v0²sin(2θ)/g; thus R=(25.0²)sin80.0°/9.80=62.8 m. (b) At 50.0°, R=(25.0²)sin100°/9.80=62.8 m, exactly equal to the 40.0° range because 40° and 50° are complementary, their doubled angles are supplementary, and sin(180°-x)=sin x.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Determine the horizontal and vertical components of the initial velocity, v0x and v0y. Before substituting any numbers, combine R = v0x t and t = 2v0y/g (with v0x = v0 cosθ, v0y = v0 sinθ) to derive the range symbolically as R = v0² sin(2θ)/g. Then use this relationship, together with H = v0y²/(2g), to calculate the maximum height, the total time of flight, and the horizontal range.\n\n(b) A second projectile is launched from the same location at the same initial speed, 25.0 m/s, but at 50.0° above the horizontal instead of 40.0°. Determine the range of this second launch and compare it to the range found in part (a). Then explain, in terms of the dependence of R on sin(2θ), why launching at 40° and at 50° (at the same speed) produces the same range. A projectile is launched from level ground at 25.0 m/s at an angle of 40.0° above the horizontal (g = 9.80 m/s^2, ignore air resistance)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0.0°", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-range", "a-symbolic-range", "a-time", "b-50deg-range", "b-symmetry-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) v0x=25.0 cos40.0° and v0y=25.0 sin40.0°. The maximum height is h=v0y²/(2g)=13.2 m, and flight time is t=2v0y/g=3.28 s. Combining R=v0x t with t=2v0y/g gives R=2v0xv0y/g=v0²sin(2θ)/g; thus R=(25.0²)sin80.0°/9.80=62.8 m. (b) At 50.0°, R=(25.0²)sin100°/9.80=62.8 m, exactly equal to the 40.0° range because 40° and 50° are complementary, their doubled angles are supplementary, and sin(180°-x)=sin x.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Determine the horizontal and vertical components of the initial velocity, v0x and v0y. Before substituting any numbers, combine R = v0x t and t = 2v0y/g (with v0x = v0 cosθ, v0y = v0 sinθ) to derive the range symbolically as R = v0² sin(2θ)/g. Then use this relationship, together with H = v0y²/(2g), to calculate the maximum height, the total time of flight, and the horizontal range.\n\n(b) A second projectile is launched from the same location at the same initial speed, 25.0 m/s, but at 50.0° above the horizontal instead of 40.0°. Determine the range of this second launch and compare it to the range found in part (a). Then explain, in terms of the dependence of R on sin(2θ), why launching at 40° and at 50° (at the same speed) produces the same range. A projectile is launched from level ground at 25.0 m/s at an angle of 40.0° above the horizontal (g = 9.80 m/s^2, ignore air resistance)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "25.0", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-range", "a-symbolic-range", "a-time", "b-50deg-range", "b-symmetry-explanation"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) v0x=25.0 cos40.0° and v0y=25.0 sin40.0°. The maximum height is h=v0y²/(2g)=13.2 m, and flight time is t=2v0y/g=3.28 s. Combining R=v0x t with t=2v0y/g gives R=2v0xv0y/g=v0²sin(2θ)/g; thus R=(25.0²)sin80.0°/9.80=62.8 m. (b) At 50.0°, R=(25.0²)sin100°/9.80=62.8 m, exactly equal to the 40.0° range because 40° and 50° are complementary, their doubled angles are supplementary, and sin(180°-x)=sin x.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Determine the horizontal and vertical components of the initial velocity, v0x and v0y. Before substituting any numbers, combine R = v0x t and t = 2v0y/g (