": "(a) The radius is r=L sinθ=(1.20)sin25.0°=0.507 m. Vertical equilibrium gives T cosθ=mg, so T=(0.500)(9.80)/cos25.0°=5.41 N. (b) Horizontally, T sinθ=mv²/r, so v=sqrt(rg tanθ)=1.52 m/s. The period is T_p=2πr/v=2π(0.507)/1.52=2.09 s.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the tension in the string and the radius of the ball's circular path.\n\n(b) Calculate the ball's speed and the period of its circular motion. A 0.500 kg ball attached to a 1.20 m string moves in a horizontal circle as a conical pendulum, with the string making a 25.0° angle with the vertical (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2.09", "criterion_keys": ["a-radius", "a-tension", "b-period", "b-speed"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) The radius is r=L sinθ=(1.20)sin25.0°=0.507 m. Vertical equilibrium gives T cosθ=mg, so T=(0.500)(9.80)/cos25.0°=5.41 N. (b) Horizontally, T sinθ=mv²/r, so v=sqrt(rg tanθ)=1.52 m/s. The period is T_p=2πr/v=2π(0.507)/1.52=2.09 s.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the tension in the string and the radius of the ball's circular path.\n\n(b) Calculate the ball's speed and the period of its circular motion. A 0.500 kg ball attached to a 1.20 m string moves in a horizontal circle as a conical pendulum, with the string making a 25.0° angle with the vertical (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-056", "content_item_id": "5f786d3e-b282-4b93-a2d2-b3218d39bdc4", "content_item_version_id": "9cac395c-05da-4b9e-bb3c-f6d3905238bf", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) On the first incline, mgh1=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.00))=6.26 m/s. On the second incline, (1/2)mv²=mgh2+μ_kmg cosθ(h2/sinθ). Hence h2=h1/[1+μ_k cotθ]=2.00/[1+(0.300)cot30.0°]=1.32 m. (b) Doubling μ_k to 0.600 gives h2=2.00/[1+(0.600)cot30.0°]=0.981 m, so the height decreases. It does not halve because h2 is proportional to 1/(sinθ+μ_k cosθ), whose denominator has the additive sinθ term, although frictional loss per meter does double.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) On the first incline, mgh1=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.00))=6.26 m/s. On the second incline, (1/2)mv²=mgh2+μ_kmg cosθ(h2/sinθ). Hence h2=h1/[1+μ_k cotθ]=2.00/[1+(0.300)cot30.0°]=1.32 m. (b) Doubling μ_k to 0.600 gives h2=2.00/[1+(0.600)cot30.0°]=0.981 m, so the height decreases. It does not halve because h2 is proportional to 1/(sinθ+μ_k cosθ), whose denominator has the additive sinθ term, although frictional loss per meter does double.", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-setup", "a-speed", "b-explanation", "b-newvalue", "b-prediction"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 6, "criteria_covered": ["a-height", "a-setup", "a-speed", "b-explanation", "b-newvalue", "b-prediction"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "1", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-setup", "a-speed", "b-explanation", "b-newvalue", "b-prediction"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) On the first incline, mgh1=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.00))=6.26 m/s. On the second incline, (1/2)mv²=mgh2+μ_kmg cosθ(h2/sinθ). Hence h2=h1/[1+μ_k cotθ]=2.00/[1+(0.300)cot30.0°]=1.32 m. (b) Doubling μ_k to 0.600 gives h2=2.00/[1+(0.600)cot30.0°]=0.981 m, so the height decreases. It does not halve because h2 is proportional to 1/(sinθ+μ_k cosθ), whose denominator has the additive sinθ term, although frictional loss per meter does double.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the first incline, then calculate the maximum height it reaches on the second incline, accounting for friction.\n\n(b) Predict how the maximum height on the second incline changes if the friction coefficient is doubled to 0.600, calculate the new maximum height, and explain the trend. A 3.00 kg block starts from rest at the top of a 2.00 m high frictionless incline, slides to the bottom, then travels up a second incline that makes a 30.0° angle with horizontal and has a kinetic friction coefficient of 0.300 (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-setup", "a-speed", "b-explanation", "b-newvalue", "b-prediction"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) On the first incline, mgh1=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.00))=6.26 m/s. On the second incline, (1/2)mv²=mgh2+μ_kmg cosθ(h2/sinθ). Hence h2=h1/[1+μ_k cotθ]=2.00/[1+(0.300)cot30.0°]=1.32 m. (b) Doubling μ_k to 0.600 gives h2=2.00/[1+(0.600)cot30.0°]=0.981 m, so the height decreases. It does not halve because h2 is proportional to 1/(sinθ+μ_k cosθ), whose denominator has the additive sinθ term, although frictional loss per meter does double.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the first incline, then calculate the maximum height it reaches on the second incline, accounting for friction.\n\n(b) Predict how the maximum height on the second incline changes if the friction coefficient is doubled to 0.600, calculate the new maximum height, and explain the trend. A 3.00 kg block starts from rest at the top of a 2.00 m high frictionless incline, slides to the bottom, then travels up a second incline that makes a 30.0° angle with horizontal and has a kinetic friction coefficient of 0.300 (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-setup", "a-speed", "b-explanation", "b-newvalue", "b-prediction"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) On the first incline, mgh1=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.00))=6.26 m/s. On the second incline, (1/2)mv²=mgh2+μ_kmg cosθ(h2/sinθ). Hence h2=h1/[1+μ_k cotθ]=2.00/[1+(0.300)cot30.0°]=1.32 m. (b) Doubling μ_k to 0.600 gives h2=2.00/[1+(0.600)cot30.0°]=0.981 m, so the height decreases. It does not halve because h2 is proportional to 1/(sinθ+μ_k cosθ), whose denominator has the additive sinθ term, although frictional loss per meter does double.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the first incline, then calculate the maximum height it reaches on the second incline, accounting for friction.\n\n(b) Predict how the maximum height on the second incline changes if the friction coefficient is doubled to 0.600, calculate the new maximum height, and explain the trend. A 3.00 kg block starts from rest at the top of a 2.00 m high frictionless incline, slides to the bottom, then travels up a second incline that makes a 30.0° angle with horizontal and has a kinetic friction coefficient of 0.300 (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "9.80", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-setup", "a-speed", "b-explanation", "b-newvalue", "b-prediction"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) On the first incline, mgh1=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.00))=6.26 m/s. On the second incline, (1/2)mv²=mgh2+μ_kmg cosθ(h2/sinθ). Hence h2=h1/[1+μ_k cotθ]=2.00/[1+(0.300)cot30.0°]=1.32 m. (b) Doubling μ_k to 0.600 gives h2=2.00/[1+(0.600)cot30.0°]=0.981 m, so the height decreases. It does not halve because h2 is proportional to 1/(sinθ+μ_k cosθ), whose denominator has the additive sinθ term, although frictional loss per meter does double.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the first incline, then calculate the maximum height it reaches on the second incline, accounting for friction.\n\n(b) Predict how the maximum height on the second incline changes if the friction coefficient is doubled to 0.600, calculate the new maximum height, and explain the trend. A 3.00 kg block starts from rest at the top of a 2.00 m high frictionless incline, slides to the bottom, then travels up a second incline that makes a 30.0° angle with horizontal and has a kinetic friction coefficient of 0.300 (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-setup", "a-speed", "b-explanation", "b-newvalue", "b-prediction"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) On the first incline, mgh1=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.00))=6.26 m/s. On the second incline, (1/2)mv²=mgh2+μ_kmg cosθ(h2/sinθ). Hence h2=h1/[1+μ_k cotθ]=2.00/[1+(0.300)cot30.0°]=1.32 m. (b) Doubling μ_k to 0.600 gives h2=2.00/[1+(0.600)cot30.0°]=0.981 m, so the height decreases. It does not hal