+(0.300)cot30.0°]=1.32 m. (b) Doubling μ_k to 0.600 gives h2=2.00/[1+(0.600)cot30.0°]=0.981 m, so the height decreases. It does not halve because h2 is proportional to 1/(sinθ+μ_k cosθ), whose denominator has the additive sinθ term, although frictional loss per meter does double.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the first incline, then calculate the maximum height it reaches on the second incline, accounting for friction.\n\n(b) Predict how the maximum height on the second incline changes if the friction coefficient is doubled to 0.600, calculate the new maximum height, and explain the trend. A 3.00 kg block starts from rest at the top of a 2.00 m high frictionless incline, slides to the bottom, then travels up a second incline that makes a 30.0° angle with horizontal and has a kinetic friction coefficient of 0.300 (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1", "criterion_keys": ["a-height", "a-setup", "a-speed", "b-explanation", "b-newvalue", "b-prediction"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) On the first incline, mgh1=(1/2)mv², so v=sqrt(2(9.80)(2.00))=6.26 m/s. On the second incline, (1/2)mv²=mgh2+μ_kmg cosθ(h2/sinθ). Hence h2=h1/[1+μ_k cotθ]=2.00/[1+(0.300)cot30.0°]=1.32 m. (b) Doubling μ_k to 0.600 gives h2=2.00/[1+(0.600)cot30.0°]=0.981 m, so the height decreases. It does not halve because h2 is proportional to 1/(sinθ+μ_k cosθ), whose denominator has the additive sinθ term, although frictional loss per meter does double.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the block's speed at the bottom of the first incline, then calculate the maximum height it reaches on the second incline, accounting for friction.\n\n(b) Predict how the maximum height on the second incline changes if the friction coefficient is doubled to 0.600, calculate the new maximum height, and explain the trend. A 3.00 kg block starts from rest at the top of a 2.00 m high frictionless incline, slides to the bottom, then travels up a second incline that makes a 30.0° angle with horizontal and has a kinetic friction coefficient of 0.300 (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-057", "content_item_id": "c5e5ce69-5365-4551-a9eb-4832cf4066f9", "content_item_version_id": "105deb51-6c66-415d-8537-28d22a54d095", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) The independent variable is horizontal launch speed (zero for the dropped ball); the dependent variable is landing time. Hold release height at 0.800 m and use identical balls released simultaneously. (b) Equal landing times despite different horizontal speeds support independence of horizontal and vertical motion. Negligible drag is essential because drag depends on total speed and would couple horizontal speed to vertical acceleration. (c) From h=(1/2)gt², t=sqrt(2h/g)=sqrt(2(0.800)/9.80)=0.404 s for both balls. Horizontal speed is absent from this vertical-motion equation.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) The independent variable is horizontal launch speed (zero for the dropped ball); the dependent variable is landing time. Hold release height at 0.800 m and use identical balls released simultaneously. (b) Equal landing times despite different horizontal speeds support independence of horizontal and vertical motion. Negligible drag is essential because drag depends on total speed and would couple horizontal speed to vertical acceleration. (c) From h=(1/2)gt², t=sqrt(2h/g)=sqrt(2(0.800)/9.80)=0.404 s for both balls. Horizontal speed is absent from this vertical-motion equation.", "criterion_keys": ["a-control1", "a-control2", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-airresistance", "b-confirmation", "c-calculation", "c-mechanism"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 8, "criteria_covered": ["a-control1", "a-control2", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-airresistance", "b-confirmation", "c-calculation", "c-mechanism"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "0.800", "criterion_keys": ["a-control1", "a-control2", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-airresistance", "b-confirmation", "c-calculation", "c-mechanism"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) The independent variable is horizontal launch speed (zero for the dropped ball); the dependent variable is landing time. Hold release height at 0.800 m and use identical balls released simultaneously. (b) Equal landing times despite different horizontal speeds support independence of horizontal and vertical motion. Negligible drag is essential because drag depends on total speed and would couple horizontal speed to vertical acceleration. (c) From h=(1/2)gt², t=sqrt(2h/g)=sqrt(2(0.800)/9.80)=0.404 s for both balls. Horizontal speed is absent from this vertical-motion equation.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design the experiment: identify the independent variable, the dependent variable, and two variables to hold constant as controls.\n\n(b) Explain what result would confirm the independence of horizontal and vertical motion, and explain why negligible air resistance is a necessary assumption for this conclusion to hold.\n\n(c) Calculate the predicted fall time for both balls, and explain, using the equation you used, why this time does not depend on horizontal launch speed. A student wants to experimentally verify that the horizontal and vertical motions of a projectile are independent by comparing a ball projected horizontally off a 0.800 m table to an identical ball simply dropped from the same height at the same instant (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1", "criterion_keys": ["a-control1", "a-control2", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-airresistance", "b-confirmation", "c-calculation", "c-mechanism"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) The independent variable is horizontal launch speed (zero for the dropped ball); the dependent variable is landing time. Hold release height at 0.800 m and use identical balls released simultaneously. (b) Equal landing times despite different horizontal speeds support independence of horizontal and vertical motion. Negligible drag is essential because drag depends on total speed and would couple horizontal speed to vertical acceleration. (c) From h=(1/2)gt², t=sqrt(2h/g)=sqrt(2(0.800)/9.80)=0.404 s for both balls. Horizontal speed is absent from this vertical-motion equation.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design the experiment: identify the independent variable, the dependent variable, and two variables to hold constant as controls.\n\n(b) Explain what result would confirm the independence of horizontal and vertical motion, and explain why negligible air resistance is a necessary assumption for this conclusion to hold.\n\n(c) Calculate the predicted fall time for both balls, and explain, using the equation you used, why this time does not depend on horizontal launch speed. A student wants to experimentally verify that the horizontal and vertical motions of a projectile are independent by comparing a ball projected horizontally off a 0.800 m table to an identical ball simply dropped from the same height at the same instant (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["a-control1", "a-control2", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-airresistance", "b-confirmation", "c-calculation", "c-mechanism"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) The independent variable is horizontal launch speed (zero for the dropped ball); the dependent va