 acceleration. (c) From h=(1/2)gt², t=sqrt(2h/g)=sqrt(2(0.800)/9.80)=0.404 s for both balls. Horizontal speed is absent from this vertical-motion equation.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design the experiment: identify the independent variable, the dependent variable, and two variables to hold constant as controls.\n\n(b) Explain what result would confirm the independence of horizontal and vertical motion, and explain why negligible air resistance is a necessary assumption for this conclusion to hold.\n\n(c) Calculate the predicted fall time for both balls, and explain, using the equation you used, why this time does not depend on horizontal launch speed. A student wants to experimentally verify that the horizontal and vertical motions of a projectile are independent by comparing a ball projected horizontally off a 0.800 m table to an identical ball simply dropped from the same height at the same instant (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "9.80", "criterion_keys": ["a-control1", "a-control2", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-airresistance", "b-confirmation", "c-calculation", "c-mechanism"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) The independent variable is horizontal launch speed (zero for the dropped ball); the dependent variable is landing time. Hold release height at 0.800 m and use identical balls released simultaneously. (b) Equal landing times despite different horizontal speeds support independence of horizontal and vertical motion. Negligible drag is essential because drag depends on total speed and would couple horizontal speed to vertical acceleration. (c) From h=(1/2)gt², t=sqrt(2h/g)=sqrt(2(0.800)/9.80)=0.404 s for both balls. Horizontal speed is absent from this vertical-motion equation.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design the experiment: identify the independent variable, the dependent variable, and two variables to hold constant as controls.\n\n(b) Explain what result would confirm the independence of horizontal and vertical motion, and explain why negligible air resistance is a necessary assumption for this conclusion to hold.\n\n(c) Calculate the predicted fall time for both balls, and explain, using the equation you used, why this time does not depend on horizontal launch speed. A student wants to experimentally verify that the horizontal and vertical motions of a projectile are independent by comparing a ball projected horizontally off a 0.800 m table to an identical ball simply dropped from the same height at the same instant (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0.404", "criterion_keys": ["a-control1", "a-control2", "a-dependent", "a-independent", "b-airresistance", "b-confirmation", "c-calculation", "c-mechanism"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) The independent variable is horizontal launch speed (zero for the dropped ball); the dependent variable is landing time. Hold release height at 0.800 m and use identical balls released simultaneously. (b) Equal landing times despite different horizontal speeds support independence of horizontal and vertical motion. Negligible drag is essential because drag depends on total speed and would couple horizontal speed to vertical acceleration. (c) From h=(1/2)gt², t=sqrt(2h/g)=sqrt(2(0.800)/9.80)=0.404 s for both balls. Horizontal speed is absent from this vertical-motion equation.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Design the experiment: identify the independent variable, the dependent variable, and two variables to hold constant as controls.\n\n(b) Explain what result would confirm the independence of horizontal and vertical motion, and explain why negligible air resistance is a necessary assumption for this conclusion to hold.\n\n(c) Calculate the predicted fall time for both balls, and explain, using the equation you used, why this time does not depend on horizontal launch speed. A student wants to experimentally verify that the horizontal and vertical motions of a projectile are independent by comparing a ball projected horizontally off a 0.800 m table to an identical ball simply dropped from the same height at the same instant (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-058", "content_item_id": "7b12123c-0fc2-4310-afd0-cae0ac31aceb", "content_item_version_id": "26b34dfd-f85b-4520-97e5-de69f04c4940", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) For the hanging mass, the free-body diagram has m2g downward and T upward; for the incline block it has T up the slope, m1g sinθ down the slope, N outward normal, and m1g cosθ into the plane. With the stated positive direction, T-m1g sinθ=m1a and m2g-T=m2a. Thus a=g(m2-m1 sinθ)/(m1+m2)=9.80(2.00-4.00 sin20.0°)/6.00=1.03 m/s², and T=m2(g-a)=2.00(9.80-1.03)=17.5 N. (b) Positive a confirms that the hanging mass moves down and the incline block moves up. At the reversal threshold m2=m1 sinθ=4.00 sin20.0°=1.37 kg. Below that mass, m1g sinθ>m2g; with no friction to hold the system, it accelerates in reverse, with the block down the incline and hanging mass up.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) For the hanging mass, the free-body diagram has m2g downward and T upward; for the incline block it has T up the slope, m1g sinθ down the slope, N outward normal, and m1g cosθ into the plane. With the stated positive direction, T-m1g sinθ=m1a and m2g-T=m2a. Thus a=g(m2-m1 sinθ)/(m1+m2)=9.80(2.00-4.00 sin20.0°)/6.00=1.03 m/s², and T=m2(g-a)=2.00(9.80-1.03)=17.5 N. (b) Positive a confirms that the hanging mass moves down and the incline block moves up. At the reversal threshold m2=m1 sinθ=4.00 sin20.0°=1.37 kg. Below that mass, m1g sinθ>m2g; with no friction to hold the system, it accelerates in reverse, with the block down the incline and hanging mass up.", "criterion_keys": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-setup", "a-tension", "b-directionmeaning", "b-reduced", "b-threshold"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 7, "criteria_covered": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-setup", "a-tension", "b-directionmeaning", "b-reduced", "b-threshold"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "9.80", "criterion_keys": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-setup", "a-tension", "b-directionmeaning", "b-reduced", "b-threshold"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) For the hanging mass, the free-body diagram has m2g downward and T upward; for the incline block it has T up the slope, m1g sinθ down the slope, N outward normal, and m1g cosθ into the plane. With the stated positive direction, T-m1g sinθ=m1a and m2g-T=m2a. Thus a=g(m2-m1 sinθ)/(m1+m2)=9.80(2.00-4.00 sin20.0°)/6.00=1.03 m/s², and T=m2(g-a)=2.00(9.80-1.03)=17.5 N. (b) Positive a confirms that the hanging mass moves down and the incline block moves up. At the reversal threshold m2=m1 sinθ=4.00 sin20.0°=1.37 kg. Below that mass, m1g sinθ>m2g; with no friction to hold the system, it accelerates in reverse, with the block down the incline and hanging mass up.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Let positive acceleration correspond to the hanging mass moving downward and the incline block moving up the incline (this defines the positive direction for the whole problem).\n\n(a) Draw a labeled free-body diagram for each object, apply Newton's second law to each mass, and calculate the acceleration of the system and the tension in the string.\n\n(b) Explain the physical meaning of the sign of the acceleration you calculated, calculate the threshold hanging mass at which the direction of motion would reverse, and explain what happens if the hanging mass is reduced below that threshold. A 4.00 kg block on a frictionless 20.0° incline is connected by a light string over a frictionless pulley at the top of the incline to a hanging 2.00 kg mass (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-setup", "a-tension", "b-directionmeaning", "b-reduced", "b-threshold"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) For the hanging mass, the free-body diagram has m2g downward and T upward; for the incline block it has T up the slope, m1g sinθ down the slope, N outward normal, and m1g cosθ into the plane. With the stated positive direction, T-m1g sinθ=m1a and m2g-T=m2a. Thus a=g(m2-m1 sinθ)/(m1+m2)=9.80(2.00-4.00 sin20.0°)/6.00=1.03 m/s², and T=m2(g-a)=2.00(9.80-1.03)=17.5 N. (b) Positive a confirms that the hanging mass moves down and the incline block moves up. At the reversal threshold m2=m1 sinθ=4.00 sin20.0°=1.37 kg. Below that mass, m1g sinθ>m2g; with no friction to hold the system, it accelerates in reverse, with the block down the incline and hanging mass up.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Let positive acceleration correspond to the hanging mass moving downward and the incline block moving up the incline (this defines the positive direction for the whole problem).\n\n(a) Draw a labeled free-body diagram for each object, apply Newton's second law to each mass, and calculate the acceleration of the system and the tension in the string.\n\n(b) Explain the physical meaning of the sign of the acceleration you calculated, calculate the threshold hanging mass at which the direction of motion would reverse, and explain what happens if the hanging mass is reduced below that threshold. A 4.00 kg block on a frictionless 20.0° incline is connected by a light string over a frictionless pulley at the top of the incline to a hanging 2.00 kg mass (g = 9.80 m/s^2)."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "4.00", "criterion_keys": ["a-accel", "a-fbd", "a-setup", "a-tension", "b-directionmeanin