ote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the object's acceleration during the interval 0 < t < 4 s.\n\n(b) Calculate the object's displacement during the interval 0 <= t <= 4 s.\n\n(c) Describe the shape of the object's position-time graph over the full 0 <= t <= 6 s interval, and explain your reasoning.\n\n(d) State the object's acceleration during the interval 4 s < t < 6 s. A velocity-time graph shows an object's velocity decreasing linearly from 4 m/s at t = 0 s to 0 m/s at t = 4 s. From t = 4 s to t = 6 s, the object's velocity remains constant at 0 m/s."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "medium", "flags": ["textual_graph_or_diagram_representation", "cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-np1-003", "content_item_id": "fb4aa77d-09d0-4a4b-824f-108b5e762a78", "content_item_version_id": "64f59454-5f26-4483-8407-7202ed6f8142", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) Vertically, 20=(1/2)(10)t², so t=2 s. (b) Horizontally, x=v_xt=(8)(2)=16 m. (c) Taking upward positive, v_y=-gt=-(10)(2)=-20 m/s. (d) After launch gravity is the only force and is vertical, so horizontal net force and acceleration are zero; therefore horizontal velocity remains 8 m/s.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) Vertically, 20=(1/2)(10)t², so t=2 s. (b) Horizontally, x=v_xt=(8)(2)=16 m. (c) Taking upward positive, v_y=-gt=-(10)(2)=-20 m/s. (d) After launch gravity is the only force and is vertical, so horizontal net force and acceleration are zero; therefore horizontal velocity remains 8 m/s.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 4, "criteria_covered": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "20", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Vertically, 20=(1/2)(10)t², so t=2 s. (b) Horizontally, x=v_xt=(8)(2)=16 m. (c) Taking upward positive, v_y=-gt=-(10)(2)=-20 m/s. (d) After launch gravity is the only force and is vertical, so horizontal net force and acceleration are zero; therefore horizontal velocity remains 8 m/s.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2.\n\n(a) Calculate the time the ball is in the air before landing.\n\n(b) Calculate the horizontal distance the ball travels before landing.\n\n(c) Calculate the vertical component of the ball's velocity the instant before it lands, and state its direction (taking upward as positive, express this as a signed value).\n\n(d) The ball's horizontal velocity is 8 m/s at launch and remains 8 m/s the instant before landing. Explain, in terms of the forces acting on the ball, why the horizontal velocity does not change during the flight. A ball is launched horizontally with an initial speed of 8 m/s from a height of 20 m above the ground. Air resistance is negligible."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Vertically, 20=(1/2)(10)t², so t=2 s. (b) Horizontally, x=v_xt=(8)(2)=16 m. (c) Taking upward positive, v_y=-gt=-(10)(2)=-20 m/s. (d) After launch gravity is the only force and is vertical, so horizontal net force and acceleration are zero; therefore horizontal velocity remains 8 m/s.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2.\n\n(a) Calculate the time the ball is in the air before landing.\n\n(b) Calculate the horizontal distance the ball travels before landing.\n\n(c) Calculate the vertical component of the ball's velocity the instant before it lands, and state its direction (taking upward as positive, express this as a signed value).\n\n(d) The ball's horizontal velocity is 8 m/s at launch and remains 8 m/s the instant before landing. Explain, in terms of the forces acting on the ball, why the horizontal velocity does not change during the flight. A ball is launched horizontally with an initial speed of 8 m/s 