", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) The downslope weight component is mg sin30°=(4)(10)(0.5)=20 N. (b) N=mg cos30°=(4)(10)(0.866)=34.64 N≈34.6 N. (c) a=F_parallel/m=20/4=5 m/s² down the incline. (d) The normal force balances only the perpendicular 34.6 N component; the unbalanced 20 N parallel component causes acceleration, so N is less than the full 40 N weight.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2, sin(30 deg) = 0.5, cos(30 deg) = 0.866.\n\n(a) Calculate the component of the block's weight directed parallel to the incline surface.\n\n(b) Calculate the magnitude of the normal force exerted on the block by the incline surface.\n\n(c) Calculate the magnitude of the block's acceleration down the incline.\n\n(d) The magnitude of the normal force found in part (b) is less than the block's weight (40 N). Explain why, in terms of the components of the gravitational force. A 4 kg block is placed on a frictionless incline that makes a 30-degree angle with the horizontal. A coordinate axis is chosen parallel to the incline surface, consistent with the block's direction of acceleration."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "40", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) The downslope weight component is mg sin30°=(4)(10)(0.5)=20 N. (b) N=mg cos30°=(4)(10)(0.866)=34.64 N≈34.6 N. (c) a=F_parallel/m=20/4=5 m/s² down the incline. (d) The normal force balances only the perpendicular 34.6 N component; the unbalanced 20 N parallel component causes acceleration, so N is less than the full 40 N weight.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2, sin(30 deg) = 0.5, cos(30 deg) = 0.866.\n\n(a) Calculate the component of the block's weight directed parallel to the incline surface.\n\n(b) Calculate the magnitude of the normal force exerted on the block by the incline surface.\n\n(c) Calculate the magnitude of the block's acceleration down the incline.\n\n(d) The magnitude of the normal force found in part (b) is less than the block's weight (40 N). Explain why, in terms of the components of the gravitational force. A 4 kg block is placed on a frictionless incline that makes a 30-degree angle with the horizontal. A coordinate axis is chosen parallel to the incline surface, consistent with the block's direction of acceleration."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-np1-006", "content_item_id": "1f266070-c443-455c-96ac-9d9f9b709aec", "content_item_version_id": "1200ff17-ed8a-433f-ae65-efd3c136bcd0", "version_num": 2, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) At the top at minimum speed, gravity alone supplies centripetal force: mg=mv²/r, so v=sqrt(gr)=sqrt((10)(1.5))=3.87 m/s. (b) At this limiting speed N=0 N. (c) At the top, any normal force points downward toward the loop center because the inside track can push the ball inward but cannot pull it. (d) At a higher speed mv²/r is larger than mg, so a positive downward normal force supplies the additional centripetal force; N is greater than zero.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) At the top at minimum speed, gravity alone supplies centripetal force: mg=mv²/r, so v=sqrt(gr)=sqrt((10)(1.5))=3.87 m/s. (b) At this limiting speed N=0 N. (c) At the top, any normal force points downward toward the loop center because the inside track can push the ball inward but cannot pull it. (d) At a higher speed mv²/r is larger than mg, so a positive downward normal force supplies the additional centripetal force; N is greater than zero.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 4, "criteria_covered": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "10", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) At the top at minimum speed, gravity alone supplies centripetal force: mg=mv²/r, so v=sqrt(gr)=sqrt((10)(1.5))=3.87 m/s. (b) At this limiting speed N=0 N. (c) At the top, any normal force points downward toward the loop center because the inside track can push the ball inward but cannot pull it. (d) At a higher speed mv²/r is larger than mg, so a positive downward normal force supplies the additional centripetal force; N is greater than zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2.\n\n(a) Calculate the minimum speed the ball must have at the top of the loop to maintain contact with the track.\n\n(b) At this minimum speed, calculate the magnitude of the normal force exerted by the track on the ball at the top of the loop.\n\n(c) When the ball's speed at the top of the loop is greater than the minimum speed found in part (a) (so the normal force is nonzero), state the direction of the normal force exerted by the track on the ball, and justify your answer.\n\n(d) Suppose the ball instead travels through the top of the loop at a speed greater than the minimum speed found in part (a). State whether the magnitude of the normal force at the top would be greater than, less than, or equal to the value found in part (b), and justify your answer. A 2 kg ball moves through a vertical circular loop of radius 1.5 m on the inside of a track."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "1.5", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) At the top at minimum speed, gravity alone supplies centripetal force: mg=mv²/r, so v=sqrt(gr)=sqrt((10)(1.5))=3.87 m/s. (b) At this limiting speed N=0 N. (c) At the top, any normal force points downward toward the loop center because the inside track can push the ball inward but cannot pull it. (d) At a higher speed mv²/r is larger than mg, so a positive downward normal force supplies the additional centripetal force; N is greater than zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2.\n\n(a) Calculate the minimum speed the ball must have at the top of the loop to maintain contact with the track.\n\n(b) At this minimum speed, calculate the magnitude of the normal force exerted by the track on the ball at the top of the loop.\n\n(c) When the ball's speed at the top of the loop is greater than the minimum speed found in part (a) (so the normal force is nonzero), state the direction of the normal force exerted by the track on the ball, and justify your answer.\n\n(d) Suppose the ball instead travels through the top of the loop at a speed greater than the minimum speed found in part (a). State whether the magnitude of the normal force at the top would be greater than, less than, or equal to the value found in part (b), and justify your answer. A 2 kg ball moves through a vertical circular loop of radius 1.5 m on the inside of a track."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "3.87", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) At the top at minimum speed, gravity alone supplies centripetal force: mg=mv²/r, so v=sqrt(gr)=sqrt((10)(1.5))=3.87 m/s. (b) At this limiting speed N=0 N. (c) At the top, any normal force points downward toward the loop center because the inside track can push the ball inward but cannot pull it. (d) At a higher speed mv²/r is larger than mg, so a positive downward normal force supplies the additional centripetal force; N is greater than zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2.\n\n(a) Calculate the minimum speed the ball must have at the top of the loop to maintain contact with the track.\n\n(b) At this minimum speed, calculate the magnitude of the normal force exerted by the track on the ball at the top of the loop.\n\n(c) When the ball's speed at the top of the loop is greater than the minimum speed found in part (a) (so the normal force is nonzero), state the direction of the normal force exerted by the track on the ball, and justify your answer.\n\n(d) Suppose the ball instead travels through the top of the loop at a speed greater than the minimum speed found in part (a). State whether the magnitude of the normal force at the top would be greater than, less than, or equal to the value found in part (b), and justify your answer. A 2 kg ball moves through a vertical circular loop of radius 1.5 m on the inside of a track."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) At the top at minimum speed, gravity alone supplies centripetal force: mg=mv²/r, so v=sqrt(gr)=sqrt((10)(1.5))=3.87 m/s. (b) At this limiting speed N=0 N. (c) At the top, any normal force points downward toward the loop center because the inside track can push the ball inward but cannot pull it. (d) At a higher speed mv²/r is larger than mg, so a positive downward normal force supplies the additional centripetal force; N is greater than zero.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2.\n\n(a) Calculate the minimum speed the ball must have at the top of the loop to maintain contact with the track.\n\n(b) At this minimum speed, calculate the magnitude of the normal force exerted by the track on the ball at the top of the loop.\n\n(c) When the ball's speed at the top of the loop is greater than the minimum speed found in part (a) (so the normal force is nonzero), state the direction of the normal force exerted by the track on the ball, and justify your answer.\n\n(d) Suppose the ball instead travels through the top of the loop at a speed greater than the minimum speed found in part (a). State whether the magnitude of the normal force at the top would be greater than, less than, or equal to the value found in part (b), and justify your answer. A 2 kg ball moves through a vertical circular loop of radius 1.5 m on the inside of a track."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-np1-007", "content_item_id": "6b0976cd-524e-4f31-a767-0367f19d50be", "content_item_version_id": "664f7767-66a4-49c6-9959-452e6c5e8666", "version_num": 1, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) For the frictionless bank, N cosθ=mg and N sinθ=mv²/r. Dividing gives tanθ=v²/(rg). (b) v²=rg tanθ=(50)(10)(0.364)=182 m²/s². (c) v=sqrt(182)=13.5 m/s. (d) At higher speed more inward force is required than the normal force at this bank angle supplies; with no friction, the car slides up and outward toward a larger radius.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) For the frictionless bank, N cosθ=mg and N sinθ=mv²/r. Dividing gives tanθ=v²/(rg). (b) v²=rg tanθ=(50)(10)(0.364)=182 m²/s². (c) v=sqrt(182)=13.5 m/s. (d) At higher speed more inward force is required than the normal force at this bank angle supplies; with no friction, the car slides up and outward toward a larger radius.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 4, "criteria_covered": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "50", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) For the frictionless bank, N cosθ=mg and N sinθ=mv²/r. Dividing gives tanθ=v²/(rg). (b) v²=rg tanθ=(50)(10)(0.364)=182 m²/s². (c) v=sqrt(182)=13.5 m/s. (d) At higher speed more inward force is required than the normal force at this bank angle supplies; with no friction, the car slides up and outward toward a larger radius.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2, tan(20 deg) = 0.364.\n\n(a) Write the two Newton's-second-law equations (vertical and horizontal/centripetal directions) for the car on this frictionless banked curve, and combine them to show that tan(theta) = v^2/(rg).\n\n(b) Calculate v^2 for the speed at which the car can travel this curve while relying on no friction.\n\n(c) Calculate the corresponding speed v.\n\n(d) Explain what would happen to the car if it traveled around this curve faster than the speed found in part (c), given that the curve is frictionless. A car travels on a frictionless, circular, banked curve of radius 50 m. The curve is banked at an angle of 20 degrees from the horizontal."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "10", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) For the frictionless bank, N cosθ=mg and N sinθ=mv²/r. Dividing gives tanθ=v²/(rg). (b) v²=rg tanθ=(50)(10)(0.364)=182 m²/s². (c) v=sqrt(182)=13.5 m/s. (d) At higher speed more inward force is required than the normal force at this bank angle supplies; with no friction, the car slides up and outward toward a larger radius.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2, tan(20 deg) = 0.364.\n\n(a) Write the two Newton's-second-law equations (vertical and horizontal/centripetal directions) for the car on this frictionless banked curve, and combine them to show that tan(theta) = v^2/(rg).\n\n(b) Calculate v^2 for the speed at which the car can travel this curve while relying on no friction.\n\n(c) Calculate the corresponding speed v.\n\n(d) Explain what would happen to the car if it traveled around this curve faster than the speed found in part (c), given that the curve is frictionless. A car travels on a frictionless, circular, banked curve of radius 50 m. The curve is banked at an angle of 20 degrees from the horizontal."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0.364", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) For the frictionless bank, N cosθ=mg and N sinθ=mv²/r. Dividing gives tanθ=v²/(rg). (b) v²=rg tanθ=(50)(10)(0.364)=182 m²/s². (c) v=sqrt(182)=13.5 m/s. (d) At higher speed more inward force is required than the normal force at this bank angle supplies; with no friction, the car slides up and outward toward a larger radius.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2, tan(20 deg) = 0.364.\n\n(a) Write the two Newton's-second-law equations (vertical and horizontal/centripetal directions) for the car on this frictionless banked curve, and combine them to show that tan(theta) = v^2/(rg).\n\n(b) Calculate v^2 for the speed at which the car can travel this curve while relying on no friction.\n\n(c) Calculate the corresponding speed v.\n\n(d) Explain what would happen to the car if it traveled around this curve faster than the speed found in part (c), given that the curve is frictionless. A car travels on a frictionless, circular, banked curve of radius 50 m. The curve is banked at an angle of 20 degrees from the horizontal."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "182", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expressi