 two Newton's-second-law equations (vertical and horizontal/centripetal directions) for the car on this frictionless banked curve, and combine them to show that tan(theta) = v^2/(rg).\n\n(b) Calculate v^2 for the speed at which the car can travel this curve while relying on no friction.\n\n(c) Calculate the corresponding speed v.\n\n(d) Explain what would happen to the car if it traveled around this curve faster than the speed found in part (c), given that the curve is frictionless. A car travels on a frictionless, circular, banked curve of radius 50 m. The curve is banked at an angle of 20 degrees from the horizontal."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "13.5", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) For the frictionless bank, N cosθ=mg and N sinθ=mv²/r. Dividing gives tanθ=v²/(rg). (b) v²=rg tanθ=(50)(10)(0.364)=182 m²/s². (c) v=sqrt(182)=13.5 m/s. (d) At higher speed more inward force is required than the normal force at this bank angle supplies; with no friction, the car slides up and outward toward a larger radius.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2, tan(20 deg) = 0.364.\n\n(a) Write the two Newton's-second-law equations (vertical and horizontal/centripetal directions) for the car on this frictionless banked curve, and combine them to show that tan(theta) = v^2/(rg).\n\n(b) Calculate v^2 for the speed at which the car can travel this curve while relying on no friction.\n\n(c) Calculate the corresponding speed v.\n\n(d) Explain what would happen to the car if it traveled around this curve faster than the speed found in part (c), given that the curve is frictionless. A car travels on a frictionless, circular, banked curve of radius 50 m. The curve is banked at an angle of 20 degrees from the horizontal."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-np1-008", "content_item_id": "184bc8b2-94c8-4965-ab70-4bc260706fe1", "content_item_version_id": "67b35097-72a4-4af6-b621-61cf15188e9f", "version_num": 1, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) Applied-force work is W_A=(15)(5)=75 J. (b) Friction work is W_f=-(6)(5)=-30 J. (c) Net work is 75-30=45 J. (d) K_i=(1/2)(3)(4²)=24 J, so K_f=K_i+W_net=24+45=69 J. (e) From 69=(1/2)(3)v², v=sqrt(46)=6.78 m/s≈6.8 m/s.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) Applied-force work is W_A=(15)(5)=75 J. (b) Friction work is W_f=-(6)(5)=-30 J. (c) Net work is 75-30=45 J. (d) K_i=(1/2)(3)(4²)=24 J, so K_f=K_i+W_net=24+45=69 J. (e) From 69=(1/2)(3)v², v=sqrt(46)=6.78 m/s≈6.8 m/s.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01", "part-e-criterion-01"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 5, "criteria_covered": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01", "part-e-criterion-01"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "15", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01", "part-e-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Applied-force work is W_A=(15)(5)=75 J. (b) Friction work is W_f=-(6)(5)=-30 J. (c) Net work is 75-30=45 J. (d) K_i=(1/2)(3)(4²)=24 J, so K_f=K_i+W_net=24+45=69 J. (e) From 69=(1/2)(3)v², v=sqrt(46)=6.78 m/s≈6.8 m/s.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the work done on the box by the applied force.\n\n(b) Calculate the work done on the box by friction.\n\n(c) Calculate the net work done on the box.\n\n(d) Using the work-energy theorem, calculate the box's final kinetic energy.\n\n(e) Calculate the box's final speed. A 3 kg box, initially moving at 4 m/s, is pushed by a constant horizontal applied force of 15 N over a distance of 5 m along a rough, horizontal floor. A constant friction force of 6 N opposes the box's motion over this distance."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "5", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01", "part-e-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Applied-force work is W_A=(15)(5)=75 J. (b) Friction work is W_f=-(6)(5)=-30 J. (c) Net work is 75-30=45 J. (d) K_i=(1/2)(3)(4²)=24 J, so K_f=K_i+W_net=24+45=69 J. (e) From 69=(1/2)(3)v², v=sqrt(46)=6.78 m/s≈6.8 m/s.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the work done on the box by the applied force.\n\n(b) Calculate the work done on the box by friction.\n\n(c) Calculate the net work done on the box.\n\n(d) Using the work-energy theorem, calculate the box's final kinetic energy.\n\n(e) Calculate the box's final speed. A 3 kg box, initially moving at 4 m/s, is pushed by a constant horizontal applied force of 15 N over a distance of 5 m along a rough, horizontal floor. A constant friction force of 6 N opposes the box's motion over this distance."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "75", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01", "part-e-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) Applied-force work is W_A=(15)(5)=75 J. (b) Friction work is W_f=-(6)(5)=-30 J. (c) Net work is 75-30=45 J. (