the block leaves the spring to the instant it reaches its maximum height, and state what happens to the system's total mechanical energy over this process. Take gravitational potential energy to be zero at the bottom of the incline, and assume the block separates from the spring once the spring reaches its natural length. A 2 kg block is placed against a spring with spring constant k = 200 N/m, which is compressed 0.3 m from its natural length. The spring is released, launching the block, which then travels up a frictionless incline. The track and incline are frictionless everywhere."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0.30", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "computed", "expression": "(a) U_s=(1/2)k(Δx)²=(1/2)(200)(0.30²)=9 J. (b) At the spring's equilibrium point, (1/2)(2)v²=9, so v=3 m/s. (c) At maximum height, mgh=9, so h=9/[(2)(10)]=0.45 m. (d) As the block rises, kinetic energy decreases from 9 J to 0 while gravitational potential energy rises from 0 to 9 J. Total mechanical energy remains 9 J because the track is frictionless and only conservative forces act.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2.\n\n(a) Calculate the elastic potential energy stored in the compressed spring.\n\n(b) Calculate the block's speed as it leaves the spring at the bottom of the incline.\n\n(c) Calculate the maximum height the block reaches on the incline.\n\n(d) Describe how the distribution of kinetic energy and gravitational potential energy in the block-spring-Earth system changes from the instant the block leaves the spring to the instant it reaches its maximum height, and state what happens to the system's total mechanical energy over this process. Ta