 elevator to lift it at this constant velocity, and explain why this force must equal the elevator's weight.\n\n(b) Calculate the mechanical power output of the motor.\n\n(c) Given that the motor operates at 80% efficiency, calculate the electrical power input required. A motor lifts a 1000 kg elevator vertically at a constant velocity of 2 m/s. Friction between the elevator and its guide rails is negligible."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "000", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) Constant velocity means a=0 and zero net force, so the lift force balances weight: F=mg=(1000)(10)=10,000 N. (b) Mechanical output power is P_out=Fv=(10,000)(2)=20,000 W. (c) With efficiency 0.80=P_out/P_in, P_in=20,000/0.80=25,000 W.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question. Use g = 10 m/s^2.\n\n(a) Calculate the force the motor must exert on the elevator to lift it at this constant velocity, and explain why this force must equal the elevator's weight.\n\n(b) Calculate the mechanical power output of the motor.\n\n(c) Given that the motor operates at 80% efficiency, calculate the electrical power input required. A motor lifts a 1000 kg elevator vertically at a constant velocity of 2 m/s. Friction between the elevator and its guide rails is negligible."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}], "confidence": "high", "flags": ["cross_criterion_entanglement"]}
{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-np2-007", "content_item_id": "9f6cd48a-7052-4eb9-817e-fd3e40d90d8f", "content_item_version_id": "01e51098-a810-4cdf-9be9-e6e98c7be0ea", "version_num": 1, "full_text": "Response:\n(a) T=2πsqrt(m/k)=2πsqrt(0.50/200)=0.314 s, and f=1/T=3.18 Hz. (b) E=(1/2)kA²=(1/2)(200)(0.10²)=1.00 J. Setting E=(1/2)mv_max² gives v_max=2.00 m/s. (c) The measured 0.157 s is a half-cycle; a period returns to the same position moving in the same direction, so T=2(0.157)=0.314 s. (d) Doubling amplitude leaves T unchanged at 0.314 s, quadruples energy to 4.00 J because E is proportional to A², and doubles maximum speed to 4.00 m/s.", "spans": [{"text": "Response:\n", "criterion_keys": [], "provenance": "authored_format", "source_field": null, "source_offset": null}, {"text": "(a) T=2πsqrt(m/k)=2πsqrt(0.50/200)=0.314 s, and f=1/T=3.18 Hz. (b) E=(1/2)kA²=(1/2)(200)(0.10²)=1.00 J. Setting E=(1/2)mv_max² gives v_max=2.00 m/s. (c) The measured 0.157 s is a half-cycle; a period returns to the same position moving in the same direction, so T=2(0.157)=0.314 s. (d) Doubling amplitude leaves T unchanged at 0.314 s, quadruples energy to 4.00 J because E is proportional to A², and doubles maximum speed to 4.00 m/s.", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "provenance": "authored", "source_field": "stem+stimulus+rubric+approved_fact_pack", "source_offset": null}], "coverage": {"criteria_total": 4, "criteria_covered": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "criteria_uncovered": []}, "derivations": [{"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) T=2πsqrt(m/k)=2πsqrt(0.50/200)=0.314 s, and f=1/T=3.18 Hz. (b) E=(1/2)kA²=(1/2)(200)(0.10²)=1.00 J. Setting E=(1/2)mv_max² gives v_max=2.00 m/s. (c) The measured 0.157 s is a half-cycle; a period returns to the same position moving in the same direction, so T=2(0.157)=0.314 s. (d) Doubling amplitude leaves T unchanged at 0.314 s, quadruples energy to 4.00 J because E is proportional to A², and doubles maximum speed to 4.00 m/s.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the period and the frequency of the block's oscillation.\n\n(b) Calculate the total mechanical energy of the block-spring system and the maximum speed of the block.\n\n(c) A student measures the time from the moment of release to the moment the block first arrives at the point 0.10 m on the opposite side of equilibrium, obtains 0.157 s, and reports this as the period. Identify the student's error and state the correct period, explaining the relationship between the measured time and the true period.\n\n(d) The experiment is repeated with the block released from rest 0.20 m from equilibrium instead. Determine what happens to the period, the total mechanical energy, and the maximum speed. Support each answer with a calculation or an explicit statement of the relevant dependence. A block of mass 0.50 kg rests on a horizontal frictionless surface and is attached to a spring of spring constant 200 N/m whose other end is fixed to a wall. The block is pulled 0.10 m from its equilibrium position and released from rest at time t = 0."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "2", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) T=2πsqrt(m/k)=2πsqrt(0.50/200)=0.314 s, and f=1/T=3.18 Hz. (b) E=(1/2)kA²=(1/2)(200)(0.10²)=1.00 J. Setting E=(1/2)mv_max² gives v_max=2.00 m/s. (c) The measured 0.157 s is a half-cycle; a period returns to the same position moving in the same direction, so T=2(0.157)=0.314 s. (d) Doubling amplitude leaves T unchanged at 0.314 s, quadruples energy to 4.00 J because E is proportional to A², and doubles maximum speed to 4.00 m/s.", "inputs": [{"label": "stored stem and stimulus", "value": "see source_quote", "source_quote": "Answer all parts of the following question.\n\n(a) Calculate the period and the frequency of the block's oscillation.\n\n(b) Calculate the total mechanical energy of the block-spring system and the maximum speed of the block.\n\n(c) A student measures the time from the moment of release to the moment the block first arrives at the point 0.10 m on the opposite side of equilibrium, obtains 0.157 s, and reports this as the period. Identify the student's error and state the correct period, explaining the relationship between the measured time and the true period.\n\n(d) The experiment is repeated with the block released from rest 0.20 m from equilibrium instead. Determine what happens to the period, the total mechanical energy, and the maximum speed. Support each answer with a calculation or an explicit statement of the relevant dependence. A block of mass 0.50 kg rests on a horizontal frictionless surface and is attached to a spring of spring constant 200 N/m whose other end is fixed to a wall. The block is pulled 0.10 m from its equilibrium position and released from rest at time t = 0."}, {"label": "governing relations", "value": "see approved fact pack", "source_quote": "docs/product/AP_PHYSICS_1_CED_FACT_PACK.md"}]}, {"value": "0.50", "criterion_keys": ["part-a-criterion-01", "part-b-criterion-01", "part-c-criterion-01", "part-d-criterion-01"], "span_index": 1, "kind": "given", "expression": "(a) T=2πsqrt(m/k)=2πsqrt(0.50/200)=0.314 s, and f=1/T=3.18 Hz. (b) E=(1/2)kA²=(1/2)(200)(0.10²)=1.00 J. Setting E=(1/2)mv_max² gives v_max=2.00 m/s. (c) The measured 0.157 s is a half-cycle; a period returns to the same position moving in the same direction, so T=2(0.157)=0.314 s. (d) 